{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjCRaWq1CGqWbqYyS1iB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GjqtrjTabt2nhqfpoL9cAU","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211935__x12d0e8/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":330},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8347,"cache_read_input_tokens":344698,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8347},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0178d0d-15a3-488a-af93-32cb0d078d37","uuid":"4ac28264-a4ad-4dad-833c-432ffbc7765c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:04:41.934Z","request_id":"req_011CgjCRaBjtWur1AcPsPm2a","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01GjqtrjTabt2nhqfpoL9cAU":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211935__x12d0e8/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":330}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GjqtrjTabt2nhqfpoL9cAU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# PLAN: a first-person open-world mercenary game inspired by Far Cry 2\n2\t\n3\t## 0. How to use this plan\n4\t\n5\t- You build the whole game from this plan and your TASK.md, alone. Nobody is available to answer questions: decide and move on. **If this plan and TASK.md disagree, TASK.md wins.**\n6\t- **Title:** yours to pick, by the rule in your TASK.md (a riff, never the source's actual name). Do that first. Nothing here depends on it. `src/data/meta.js` exports `TITLE` and `TAGLINE`, and every screen and tag reads them.\n7\t- **Names:** your TASK.md gives the dev folder's name, the deliverable HTML's name and the exit-interview file's name, so this plan never repeats them. Every path below is relative to the dev folder.\n8\t- **✔ verified** means the planner ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04 with `three@0.180.0`, `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3` and Chromium 153. Don't re-research those. Further research is optional; report whatever you do in Source Information.\n9\t- Everything in this plan is in scope. §15 lists the only things that may shrink, and they shrink in quantity, never in kind. Nothing ships half-built: if something is cut, it's absent, not stubbed.\n10\t- The \"This computer\" section of your TASK.md still applies. §14.1 lists where the planner found it needs a correction.\n11\t\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## 1. The game on one page\n15\t\n16\t**Pitch.** You are a foreign mercenary in the last dry season of a dying company-state. You came for one clerk and his ledger. You stay because both sides pay on time. The grass burns where the wind takes it, your gun rusts in your hands, the fever comes back on its own schedule, and the friend who dragged you out of the fire yesterday may need you to end it for them tomorrow.\n17\t\n18\t| Decision | Choice | Why |\n19\t|---|---|---|\n20\t| Genre | Open-world first-person shooter with stealth, survival friction and a branching contract story | That is what makes the source distinct: a place that pushes back |\n21\t| Perspective | First-person, always, in play and in vehicles, with no cutscene camera. NPCs talk to you in the world while you stand, walk or drive. Attract mode alone may use chase and fly-by cameras | \"Stay in your own eyes\" is half of the source's feel |\n22\t| Players | Campaign for 1. A co-op job for 1–4 in AI Hub sessions and on its own (§6) | TASK.md's multiplayer rule |\n23\t| Length | Critical path about 45–60 minutes. All features are seen by about minute 35 | Long enough to show everything |\n24\t\n25\t**Pillars** (when in doubt, these decide):\n26\t\n27\t1. **The land is the weapon.** Wind, dry grass, slope, time of day. The best plan in the game is a flare and a good read of the wind, and the wind can turn.\n28\t2. **Nothing is free, nothing lasts.** Guns rust, trucks break, pills run out, friends die for good. This friction is the drama, so it must always be readable and answerable, never a chore.\n29\t3. **Stay in your own eyes.** Map in your hand, pliers in your arm, papers held out of the truck window. Diegetic first. HUD only for what a couch player can't otherwise read.\n30\t4. **Everybody's money is dirty.** Every contract has a cost you can later see on the map and in the camp.\n31\t5. **Read it from the couch.** At 10 feet, big, few and clear beats realistic and detailed, every time.\n32\t\n33\t**What we keep from the source's design, and what we fix:**\n34\t\n35\t| Keep (as our own version) | Fix |\n36\t|---|---|\n37\t| Spreading grass fire driven by wind | Fire is readable: a cloth strip on your gun and the truck's aerial shows the wind |\n38\t| Guns that wear out and jam; a dealer who sells reliable ones | Condition shows on the gun itself as rust and tape, so a jam is never a surprise you couldn't have seen coming |\n39\t| Recurring fever and pills earned through errands | The first attack never lands mid-fight, and warnings are generous. Later ones can land anywhere |\n40\t| Map and GPS held in your hand, usable on the move and at the wheel | At 10 feet the map can be pulled up to fill the screen |\n41\t| Painful first-person field surgery | Clear, short, interruptible, with several variations |\n42\t| A buddy who drags you out; permanent buddy death; mercy | Rescue is limited per day and telegraphed, so the player can plan around it |\n43\t| Two corrupt factions; buddies offering a better, riskier way to do a job | Consequences stay visible on the map and in the camp |\n44\t| Hidden diamonds found with a locator on the GPS | Same, with light, beep and rumble |\n45\t| Sleep to set the time of day; night is for sneaking | Same, as a watch dial |\n46\t| **Not kept:** guard posts that refill the moment you leave | Cleared posts stay cleared until the next dawn, and only refill if a supply route survives. A real truck brings the men |\n47\t| **Not kept:** every vehicle attacks on sight, always | Faction papers let you bluff through that faction's checkpoints: a real tense scene at the truck window |\n48\t\n49\t**Originality rules (hard).** This is an original game in the source's genre and spirit. Use none of its names, characters, story beats, map, dialogue, music, art or logos: no Jackal, no APR or UFLL, no Pala or Bowa-Seko, no taxi-ride opening, no villain at your bedside. Don't quote its lines or the philosophers it quotes. Our fiction, cast, map and rhetoric (§2, §5) are our own.\n50\t\n51\t**Respect rules (hard).** No real country, continent, ethnic group or language is named or imitated. Locals are people with jobs, jokes and plans, never scenery. Their names are job titles because everyone's are (§2). The game's contempt points at the trade and at the foreigners, you included. No child soldiers, no sexual violence, no torture scenes, no real-world atrocities. Violence is inked and stylised: black ink spatter, charred silhouettes, no gore models.\n52\t\n53\t---\n54\t\n55\t## 2. The world: Concession Fourteen\n56\t\n57\t**Premise.** The country was never a country. It was a mining company's private territory, leased by the square mile: Concession Fourteen. The company folded and left behind its pit, its rail line, its security force and its union. Those last two are now at war, and they still use the company's job titles because names get you killed. *Only foreigners and the dead keep their names here.* The flag is the old company logo (a pick crossed with a pen inside a diamond), painted over by both sides.\n58\t\n59\t**Factions** (both are awful, both are funny, neither is ever \"the good one\"):\n60\t\n61\t- **The Stewardship (\"Stewards\").** Ex-company security holding the rail, the paperwork and the euphemisms. Ochre armbands, khaki. Speak like a quarterly report. Led locally by **the Comptroller**.\n62\t- **The Common Shift (\"the Commons\").** Ex-miners' union holding the diesel, the numbers and the grudges. Red armbands, work clothes. Speak like a shop-floor meeting at gunpoint. Led locally by **the Foreman**.\n63\t- **The Underwriters.** The foreign insurers who have written policies for both sides. Seen only as one agent in a clean shirt on the barge. They hired you.\n64\t\n65\t**Cast** (role-names; each has a fixed voice seed, §12):\n66\t\n67\t| Who | Where | What they are |\n68\t|---|---|---|\n69\t| The Ferryman | The barge | Takes you in. Ferries you between landings. Greets everyone with \"First time?\" |\n70\t| The Widow | Her yard outside Kiln | Arms dealer. Takes diamonds only. Has outlasted four governments |\n71\t| The Comptroller | Old company HQ, Kiln | Stewards' fixer. Never says \"war\" |\n72\t| The Foreman | Union hall, Kiln | Commons' fixer. Third generation in the pit |\n73\t| The Doctor | Dispensary, Kiln | Pays for favours in quinine bark. Runs papers for the people the war made homeless |\n74\t| The Clerk of Second Water | Refugee camp | Keeps the camp's count. The only honest ledger in the game |\n75\t| **The Notary** | Unknown until Act 3 | The company's last clerk. Sells to both sides and writes everything down. Your target. Heard on six dictation tapes. Forty years of pit dust in his lungs |\n76\t\n77\t**The mercs.** Four came in on the same barge. You pick one in the prologue. The other three become your **buddies** in the campaign. In co-op, seats map to them in order. Each has their own first-person hands (skin tone, tattoo or glove) and one small perk.\n78\t\n79\t| Merc | Who | Perk | Buddy role in contracts |\n80\t|---|---|---|---|\n81\t| **Ines Calloway** | Struck-off field medic. Gentle, dry, terrifying | Syringes heal 25% faster to use | Knows who's hurt and who's hiding |\n82\t| **Tomasz Wren** | Sapper, 50s, deaf in one ear, loud | Repairs and charges 25% faster | Likes it when things fall down |\n83\t| **Dao Renard** | Ex-diamond courier and bush pilot, nervy, always owed money | Locator range +50% | Knows where the money moves |\n84\t| **Sefton Bray** | Oldest merc in the Concession, here for the first war too | Guns wear 25% slower | Knows who owes whom, and why |\n85\t\n86\t**The map.** 2048 m × 2048 m, centred on the origin, y up, x east, z south. Fixed design, the same every game (no random layout). Places have merc nicknames, written in grease pencil on the map. All coordinates are metres. Adjust locally for fit, and keep the table in `src/data/world.js` as the single source of truth.\n87\t\n88\t| Id | Place | x, z | What's there |\n89\t|---|---|---|---|\n90\t| `kiln` | **Kiln**, ceasefire town | −60, 110 | 16–20 buildings, market, both fixers, the Doctor, the bar (\"The Last Invoice\"). Weapons auto-holster. Gates at N and E, both flagged |\n91\t| `landing` | **The Landing** | −55, 212 | Barge jetty on the Marrow. Prologue ends here |\n92\t| `yard` | **The Widow's Yard** | 40, 70 | Shipping containers outside the east gate. The gun table |\n93\t| `bridge_road` | **Kiln Bridge** | 130, 60 | Old road bridge east across the Marrow, the only road to the east bank. South-east to the Long Grass, north-east to the Workings |\n94\t| `s1` | Safehouse **Pump House** | −240, −40 | Given in C1 |\n95\t| `s2` | Safehouse **Salt Shed** | 420, 300 | In the Long Grass. Must be taken (C4) |\n96\t| `s3` | Safehouse **Signal Box** | 120, −395 | On the rail line. Optional: clear its guards and it's yours (Salt Shed is the same, through C4) |\n97\t| `gp1` | Post **Junction** | 40, −120 | Crossroads north of Kiln. Commons at start |\n98\t| `gp2` | Post **Grass Road** | 300, 180 | Tank stand, sandbags. Commons at start |\n99\t| `gp3` | Post **Rail Crossing** | 120, −300 | Stewards at start |\n100\t| `gp4` | Post **Depot Road** | −420, −300 | Stewards at start |\n101\t| `gp5` | Post **Workings Gate** | 570, −370 | East bank, where the road and rail meet the pit's fence. Stewards at start |\n102\t| `gp6` | Post **Switchback Foot** | −300, −540 | Foot of the only road up the Escarpment. Nobody's: deserters from both |\n103\t| `gp7` | Post **Low Ford** | −500, 390 | Vehicle ford toward the camp. Commons at start |\n104\t| `gp8` | Post **North Road** | 380, −60 | East bank, on the road from Kiln Bridge to the Workings. Stewards at start |\n105\t| `dump` | **The Fuel Dump** | 560, 520 | Commons diesel tanks, office, in the deepest grass |\n106\t| `railbridge` | **Bad Bridge** | 470, −380 | Steel rail span over the Marrow |\n107\t| `depot` | **West Depot** | −780, −380 | Stewards rail yard, supply base |\n108\t| `workings` | **The Workings** | 760, −430 | East bank. Terraced open pit, about 180 m radius, 40 m deep. Conveyor, sheds, strongroom at the bottom |\n109\t| `mission` | **The Mission** | −420, −820 | Old church and radio mast on the Escarpment. Commons and Stewards both avoid it |\n110\t| `camp` | **Second Water** | −600, 500 | Refugee camp, south bank |\n111\t| `waterhole` | **The Waterhole** | 700, 120 | Herds gather here |\n112\t\n113\t**Terrain.** A pure function `heightAt(x, z)` in `src/sim/terrain.js`, baked to a Float32Array at 2 m (1025 × 1025): low rolling noise (±8 m), then authored features from data.\n114\t\n115\t- **The Marrow:** a spline through (900, −1024), (760, −740), (540, −560), (470, −380), (400, −220), (200, −60), (60, 180), (−120, 290), (−420, 360), (−760, 470), (−1024, 720). Carved 6 m deep, 30–60 m wide. It splits the map: Kiln, the Depot and the Escarpment on the west bank; the Long Grass, the Dump, the Workings and Second Water on the east and south.\n116\t- **The Escarpment:** +80 m plateau north-west of the line from (−1024, −420) to (120, −780), with a single switchback road.\n117\t- **The Workings:** a terraced negative cone.\n118\t- Flat disks under POIs, and 7 m flat corridors along road splines.\n119\t- **Rail line:** from `depot` along z ≈ −380, past Signal Box, over `railbridge`, to the pit's west side.\n120\t- **Roads:** Kiln north gate → Junction → Rail Crossing → Depot Road → Switchback Foot → Mission. Kiln east gate → Yard → Kiln Bridge, then forking to Grass Road → Dump and to North Road → Workings Gate. A west road from Kiln to Low Ford → Second Water.\n121\t- **Biome mask:** tall grass (1.3 m) over the Long Grass quadrant (x > 130, z > 80) and in patches; short grass and bare laterite elsewhere; rock on the Escarpment; green strip along the river.\n122\t- **Scatter (seeded, deterministic):** about 1500 flat-topped acacias, 4000 brush clumps, 800 rocks, 120 termite mounds. Trees and rocks are static colliders.\n123\t\n124\t**Time.** 24 game hours = 36 real minutes by default (tunable). Sleep skips to a chosen time. Dawn, morning, noon, golden hour, dusk and night all look clearly different (§10.3) and change AI vision.\n125\t\n126\t**Wind.** A global angle and speed (0–12 m/s), drifting slowly on seeded noise, with gust events and scripted shifts. Read it from the cloth strip on your weapon and aerial, the grass sway, the smoke lean, and the GPS arrow.\n127\t\n128\t**Weather.** Clear, dust haze, and dry thunder (lightning can start far-off fires). One scripted rain squall in Ending 3's aftermath. Rain puts fires out, and it should feel like an event because the season is dry.\n129\t\n130\t**Ambient life (not optional, it's half the mood).**\n131\t\n132\t- Zebra and antelope herds that flee gunfire and fire.\n133\t- Vultures that circle where bodies are.\n134\t- Dust devils, river mist at dawn, distant artillery flashes at night.\n135\t- Civilians in Kiln and the camp going about their day, and scattering when shots start.\n136\t- Painted road signs at junctions pointing to places (diegetic navigation).\n137\t- Slogans on walls (\"SHIFT'S NOT OVER\", \"STEWARDS KEEP WHAT THEY STEAL\") and company notices (`NOTICE: LEASE TERMS UNCHANGED`).\n138\t\n139\t---\n140\t\n141\t## 3. Loop and pacing\n142\t\n143\t**Core loop:** wake at a safehouse and choose your hour → take a contract (a buddy may call with a better, riskier way) → cross the country with the map in your hand, past posts and patrols → read the ground through the monocular (wind, grass, guards) → do the job your way (burn, shoot, sneak, drive through it) → survive the friction (jam, fever, wound, breakdown) → get paid in diamonds and spend them → see what your work changed → sleep.\n144\t\n145\t**Pacing target for a first play:**\n146\t\n147\t| Minute | Beat | Features first seen |\n148\t|---|---|---|\n149\t| 0–4 | Prologue on the barge | First-person dialogue, merc pick, map, checkpoint and papers |\n150\t| 4–12 | C1 First Wages | Truck, GPS, post fight, forced jam, loot, surgery, shop, locator, safehouse, sleep and save |\n151\t| 12–22 | C2 Dry Grass | Wind, flare, fire, monocular, machete, twist, a buddy rescue if you go down |\n152\t| ~17 | First fever | Warp, pill. Doctor's errand offered |\n153\t| 22–30 | E1 Bark run | Skiff, ferry, camp, consequences |\n154\t| 30–38 | C3 Bad Bridge | Night, charges, explosion physics, train |\n155\t| 38–44 | C4/C5/C6 in player's order | Safehouse capture, convoy, mounted gun, dynamite truck, forged papers |\n156\t| 44–52 | C7 The Workings | Choose a side. Big battle. Mercy |\n157\t| 52–60 | C8 The Notary | Three endings |\n158\t\n159\t**The Director (`src/sim/director.js`) guarantees the first-time moments** so every player sees everything:\n160\t\n161\t| Event | Rule |\n162\t|---|---|\n163\t| First jam | Forced on the first firefight, about the 15th shot, with the C1 starter gun |\n164\t| First breakdown | Scripted engine hit on the road after C1 |\n165\t| First fever | 5 minutes after C1 ends, only when no enemy is alerted within 40 m |\n166\t| First rescue | Guaranteed on the first time you go down, whichever buddy is available |\n167\t| Mercy | C7 (§5) |\n168\t| Fire near you | C2's wind shift is scripted once |\n169\t\n170\tAfter a feature's first use, a one-line tip goes in the notebook.\n171\t\n172\t**Feature list (F-list).** `src/data/features.js` lists every feature id. The sim records each as seen. Tests assert the scripted playthrough covers 100% and attract mode covers at least 80% (§14). Ids:\n173\t\n174\t`fire_spread wind_shift flare_ignite molotov torch_flame rocket explosion_physics tree_burn ember_spot regrow jam jam_clear gun_break rust_visual loot_gun shop_buy armoury_redraw heal_syringe surgery_bullet surgery_finger surgery_shrapnel surgery_burn fever pill collapse rescue buddy_down mercy twist_offer twist_done papers_ok papers_fail post_clear post_regarrison monocular_tag machete_takedown crouch_grass night_stealth map_raise map_focus map_drive gps_waypoint locator_found diamond dictation drive breakdown repair mounted_gun skiff ferry truck_cargo train sleep_time save_load day_night ceasefire_town ragdoll wounded_crawl comrade_drag animals_flee ending_read ending_sell ending_burn coop_revive coop_case`\n175\t\n176\t---\n177\t\n178\t## 4. Systems\n179\t\n180\tAll numbers are defaults. Everything in the Tunables table (§9) reads from `tunables` live. The sim is deterministic: fixed 60 Hz step, seeded RNG streams, no `Math.random` (§13).\n181\t\n182\t### 4.1 Player\n183\t\n184\t- Walk 4.2 m/s, sprint 7.0 (6 s stamina, 4 s regen), crouch 2.2, jump 1.1 m. Eye height 1.62 standing, 1.05 crouched. FOV 75 (setting), 50 when aimed.\n185\t- **Health:** 100, shown as 5 big bars. Damage regenerates only up to the top of the current bar, 4 s after the last hit, at 6 hp/s.\n186\t- **Downed** at 0. See rescue, §4.11. With no rescue available you die: a ledger page (\"Contract void.\") and a reload from the last save.\n187\t- Rapier kinematic character controller (recipe §13.5). Legs visible when you look down. Head bob is subtle and switchable.\n188\t\n189\t### 4.2 Weapons, condition and jams\n190\t\n191\tLoadout: **Sidearm + Long Gun + Heavy**, plus machete and Molotovs. Merc nicknames, no real brand names. Each weapon instance has its own **condition 0–100**.\n192\t\n193\t| Id | Name | Slot | RPM | Dmg | Mag / reserve | Eff. range | Wear/shot | Price | Notes |\n194\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n195\t| `pistol` | Service Pistol | Side | 300 | 24 | 12 / 48 | 30 m | 0.10 | start | C1's worn starter |\n196\t| `quiet` | Quiet Pistol | Side | 240 | 22 | 10 / 30 | 22 m | 0.14 | 3 | Heard at 12 m |\n197\t| `flare` | Flare Pistol | Side | 30 | 12 | 1 / 8 | arc 80 m | 0.5 | 2 (free in C2) | Ignites fuel within 2 m of impact; lights the dark |\n198\t| `carbine` | Worker's Carbine | Long | 600 | 28 | 30 / 120 | 60 m | 0.05 | 3 | Commonest loot |\n199\t| `rifle` | Surveyor's Rifle | Long | 45 | 110 | 5 / 20 | 400 m | 0.35 | 6 | Bolt, 4× scope |\n200\t| `pump` | Trench Pump | Long | 65 | 13 × 9 | 6 / 24 | 18 m | 0.25 | 4 | |\n201\t| `belt` | Belt Gun | Heavy | 700 | 26 | 80 / 160 | 70 m | 0.04 | 7 | You walk slowly |\n202\t| `tube` | The Tube | Heavy | 1 shot | 180 splash 6 m | 1 / 4 | 150 m | 1.5 | 8 | Ignites |\n203\t| `torch` | Drip Torch | Heavy | stream | 40 dps | 100 / 200 fuel | 14 m | 0.1/s | 6 | Ignites. Tank can explode if shot |\n204\t\n205\t- **Machete:** 60 dmg, instant silent kill from behind (prompt), no wear.\n206\t- **Molotov:** carry 3, 5 m burn patch, usable from a truck window.\n207\t- **Condition:**\n208\t  - Dealer-fresh 100. Looted 18–55.\n209\t  - Jam chance per shot: `jamK × ((50 − cond) / 50)²` below condition 50, with `jamK` 0.04 by default.\n210\t  - Condition drops 2 when dropped in water or caught in fire.\n211\t- **Jam:**\n212\t  - The gun clicks, a jam sound plays, and the reload button clears it: hands slap and rack, 1.0–1.8 s by weapon class.\n213\t  - Below condition 15 a clear can fail (15%).\n214\t  - At condition 0 the next jam **breaks** the gun: it comes apart in your hands and is gone.\n215\t- **Reading it:** condition drives a `rust` uniform on the view model (noise-thresholded rust blotches, tape wraps below 30, wisp of smoke below 10). No HUD number; the HUD ammo icon only cracks below 25.\n216\t- **Shop model:** buying unlocks a weapon for good. A fresh copy can then be drawn free from any **armoury crate** (the Yard, and safehouses once bought).\n217\t\n218\t**Targets:** enemy guard TTK with carbine about 4 body hits, 1–2 head. Player survives about 8 enemy carbine hits (14 each) from full. Standing in the open at 30 m under 3 guards takes about 1.5 hits/s. Crouched in cover, far less.\n219\t\n220\t### 4.3 Wounds and healing\n221\t\n222\t- **Syringe** (carry 3, pouch upgrade +2, med boxes +1): heals fully in 1.6 s.\n223\t- **Critical** (≤ 1 bar): limp, ink closing in at the screen edges. The heal button now does **field surgery**, free, 2.6–3.5 s, interruptible, chosen by the last damage type. It restores to 2 bars. Four animations: pliers dig a bullet from the forearm (`surgery_bullet`), snap a dislocated finger back (`surgery_finger`), pull shrapnel from the thigh (`surgery_shrapnel`), wrap and bite down on a burn (`surgery_burn`). Each has its own grunt and its own rumble. Keep them short, stylised and memorable.\n224\t\n225\t### 4.4 The fever\n226\t\n227\t- You have malaria-like fever from day one. Never name a real drug beyond \"bark\" (quinine bark).\n228\t- First attack per the Director. Then every 11 ± 2 minutes of play (tunable), anywhere.\n229\t- **Warning, 35 s:**\n230\t  - Warp and desaturate in the post chain.\n231\t  - Ink \"sweat\" running at the screen edges.\n232\t  - Aim sway, ringing in the ears, heartbeat in the rumble.\n233\t  - A small fever icon on the HUD plus the prompt \"Take your dose\".\n234\t- **Last 10 s:** stumbles, flicker blackouts, no sprint.\n235\t- **Pill** (button, 1.8 s, shake the bottle and swallow dry) ends it, with effects fading over 8 s. Start with 2 doses; a bottle is 4; max 8.\n236\t- **No pill: collapse.** Black, a ledger line. You wake 6 hours later at the nearest safehouse, weapon condition −20, one diamond missing. Never a game over.\n237\t- Pills come **only** from the Doctor's errands (E1, E2), and the Doctor gives 2 doses free when you're at zero after a collapse, with a line that makes you feel it. So there is always a way forward.\n238\t\n239\t### 4.5 Fire and wind (the star; build it early)\n240\t\n241\t**Grid:** 512 × 512 cells, 4 m each, typed arrays in `src/sim/fire.js`: `fuel` (0 none, 1 grass, 2 brush, 3 tree, 4 structure), `moist` (0–1), `state` (0 unburnt, 1 burning, 2 burnt), `timer`. Index `iz * 512 + ix`, with `ix = floor((x + 1024) / 4)`. Roads, rock and water have fuel 0. Tick at 10 Hz over a burning set only.\n242\t\n243\t**Spread:** each burning cell tries each of 8 neighbours with chance\n244\t`spreadBase × fuelK × windFactor × slopeK × (1 − moist) × dt`,\n245\twhere `windFactor = 1 + windK × speedNorm × max(0, dot(windDir, dirToNeighbour))`, or `upwindDamp` when the dot is negative. `slopeK` is greater than 1 uphill.\n246\t\n247\t**Asserted targets** (a test measures these):\n248\t- Dead calm: front ≤ 1 m/s.\n249\t- 10 m/s wind: downwind ≥ 4 m/s (faster than walking, slower than a sprint or a truck).\n250\t- Upwind ≤ 0.3 m/s.\n251\t\n252\t**Burn times:** grass 6–10 s, brush 14 s, tree 40 s. Burning trees throw **embers**, starting spot fires up to 30 m downwind, so a one-cell road can be jumped in strong wind and a river can't.\n253\t\n254\t**Effects:**\n255\t- In or within 2 m of a burning cell: 18 hp/s and a `burning` status for 3 s. NPCs catch, panic-run (spreading it) and fall as charred silhouettes.\n256\t- Barrels and fuel tanks cook off after 3–6 s. Ammo crates pop. Vehicles take heat damage and can blow.\n257\t- NPCs treat burning cells as walls and flee fire fronts; a fire flushes men out of cover.\n258\t- A coarse smoke grid (16 m) blocks AI sight and yours.\n259\t\n260\t**Persistence:**\n261\t- Burnt ground stays black. It's saved (RLE).\n262\t- Green shoots appear after 1 game day, full grass after 3.\n263\t- Burn scars smudge the paper map as charcoal.\n264\t\n265\t**Ignition sources:** flare pistol, Molotov, Drip Torch, The Tube, explosions, burning vehicles, muzzle flash of heavy guns in very dry grass (rare), lightning, Steward fire teams.\n266\t\n267\t### 4.6 Stealth and AI\n268\t\n269\t- **Perception:** vision cone 110°, range 70 m by day, 30 m at night (the player's flashlight or a nearby fire raises it). A detection score fills by distance × light × movement × stance. Crouching in tall grass ×0.35. Smoke blocks. Hearing: carbine 180 m, quiet pistol 12 m, truck 90 m, sprint 12 m.\n270\t- **The HUD shows detection as ink arcs around the crosshair** pointing at whoever is noticing you. Nothing else.\n271\t- **States:** idle/patrol → suspicious (looks, barks) → investigate → alert (calls squad) → combat (cover, flank, suppress, rush) → flee-fire → wounded → dead.\n272\t- **Wounded:** some shot men fall, crawl and beg. A comrade may drag them (`comrade_drag`). They bleed out, or you end it.\n273\t- **Night:** some guards sleep at posts, others carry lamps.\n274\t- **NPC guns jam too** (visible fumbling): an opening for the player.\n275\t- **Implementation:**\n276\t  - A* on a 4 m grid, shared with fire (burning cells are blocked).\n277\t  - Hand-placed cover points per POI in data.\n278\t  - Rapier character controllers within 150 m of a player. Beyond that a cheap 2 Hz \"far\" mode using `heightAt` (✔ 200 controllers plus 200 dynamic bodies run at 93 steps/s in Node here, so far NPCs must not use them).\n279\t  - Line of sight by `castRay` excluding the caster (✔ rays hit your own capsule otherwise).\n280\t- **Archetypes:** guard, sergeant (150 hp, calls flanks), gunner (mounted guns and Belt Gun), fire team (Drip Torch, lights grass on purpose, late game and The Job).\n281\t- **Patrol trucks:** 2–3 per faction on road splines. Chase when alerted, with a gunner firing, and stop 30 m out to dismount. Papers apply to them too (a wave, a slow-down, or a fight).\n282\t- **Death:** hit reaction → ragdoll (§11).\n283\t\n284\t### 4.7 Posts, papers and the ceasefire\n285\t\n286\t- **Post:** 3–5 guards, sandbags, an MG nest or a parked technical, stacked barrels, grass around it you can burn.\n287\t- **Clearing a post** keeps it cleared until the next 06:00, and it only refills if its faction's depot is intact and the road to it isn't cut. Then a **real truck drives in** with 60% of its strength, reaching 100% after two dawns. `dump` burned hurts the Commons; `railbridge` blown hurts the Stewards. Contracts can flip a post's owner.\n288\t- **Papers.** Doing a faction's first contract gives you its papers (a physical card). At that faction's posts, if you approach slowly (< 25 km/h) with the gun lowered, a guard flags you down and walks to your window. You hold the papers out (`papers_ok`). He looks, says something tired, and waves you on. Speed through, or have standing below −10 or contraband in the truck: \"Out of the truck.\" (`papers_fail`), and it's a fight. Other factions' posts shoot on sight. Attacking a faction's post where witnesses survive burns your papers until the next dawn.\n289\t- **Kiln ceasefire** (Second Water too). Weapons holster at the gates, and drawing takes a deliberate long press. Fire a shot in town and both sides' gate guards turn hostile for a game day, the fixers refuse you, and the Widow charges +1.\n290\t\n291\t### 4.8 Vehicles (all Rapier)\n292\t\n293\t- **Pickup** (raycast vehicle, ✔ §13.5), **Technical** (pickup with mounted MG; switch seat with Triangle and the wheel is left free), **Cargo truck** (AI convoys; the dynamite truck in C6), **Skiff** (dynamic body with custom buoyancy and thrust, on the Marrow), **Barge** (scripted, the ferry), **Train** (kinematic on the rail spline, C3).\n294\t- First-person at the wheel with hands on it, working dash, aerial with cloth strip, and the map usable while driving.\n295\t- **Damage:** 300 hp pickup. Below 60% light smoke; below 35% engine cough and −40% top speed; below 15% stall; below 5% burning, blows in 6 s.\n296\t- **Repair:** get out, hold Use at the hood: hood opens, wrench on engine, 4.5 s, restores to 70%. Interruptible. Full repair at safehouses and the Yard.\n297\t- **Ferry:** the Ferryman runs between the Landing, Bridge Pier (near `railbridge`) and Second Water. You sit on the barge, an ink wipe passes time, you're there. A short line each trip. This is the game's fast travel.\n298\t\n299\t### 4.9 Map, GPS, locator, monocular\n300\t\n301\t- **Map (D-pad Up / M):** a physical paper map raised in your left hand, GPS clipped to it. You can walk, run or drive with it up.\n302\t- **Map content:** a CanvasTexture, hand-inked: contours, roads, rail, the Marrow, place names in grease pencil, current contract circled in red, safehouses, posts you've seen with their owner, burn scars redrawn as charcoal, your position as a small red arrow.\n303\t- **Focus (Cross, or left-click, while raised):** the map fills 85% of the screen. Left stick or mouse moves a cursor. R2/L2 or wheel zooms. Cross or left-click sets a waypoint. Square or right-click clears it. Circle or Esc lowers it. Big text only, no pins legend. At the wheel, focusing takes your hands off: the truck coasts and brakes gently until you lower it.\n304\t- **GPS:** big arrow to the waypoint, distance, wind arrow.\n305\t- **Locator:** when a diamond case is within range (120 m, upgradeable), an LED on the GPS blinks with a rising beep and a rumble tick. Faster when closer and facing it, solid green when in line.\n306\t- **Monocular (D-pad Left / B):** zoom 6×. Hold on a guard for 0.6 s to tag them with a small ink triangle shown while in line of sight. Also tags barrels and tanks.\n307\t\n308\t### 4.10 Diamonds, the Widow, safehouses\n309\t\n310\t- **Diamonds** are the only currency. 12 cases are hidden around the map (1–2 each, mostly in places worth seeing: the capsized barge, the water tower at Grass Road, under the rail bridge, the pit's upper terrace).\n311\t- **The gun table.** The Widow's stock lies on a long table in the world. Look at a gun or click it (hover or look moves the focus, with a big card: name, one line, price). Interact to buy. L1/R1 steps through items for pad players who don't want to aim at a table. No grid screens, ever.\n312\t- **Shop upgrades:** Syringe pouch 4, Molotov bandolier 3, Locator range 3, Armoury crate at Salt Shed 2, Armoury crate at Signal Box 2.\n313\t- **Economy target** (printed by `tools/balance.mjs`): contracts pay 3, 6, 7, 3, 6, 7, 10, with twists +3 each and cases about 18. Total about 75 against about 53 in the shop, so a thorough player buys most things but not early.\n314\t- **Safehouses:** a bed, med box, ammo, and an armoury crate where bought. A truck spawns outside if you came on foot. **Bed:** lie down, a large watch fills the view, and you pick Dawn, Noon, Dusk or Midnight. The hands spin and light sweeps through the shutters (ink-wipe time-lapse). Sleeping saves.\n315\t- **Saving:** one save plus one backup in `localStorage`. Autosaves when a contract starts or finishes. \"Continue\" loads the latest. No slot screens.\n316\t\n317\t### 4.11 Buddies, rescue, mercy\n318\t\n319\t- Each buddy has `trust` (starts at 30) and a state: `ok`, `wounded` (until the next sleep) or `dead` (forever). They wait at the bar, and turn up in the world for rescues and twists. Their voices and hands are as in §2.\n320\t- **Twist.** After you accept a contract, a buddy radios or meets you with a better, riskier way: an extra objective, more pay, +10 trust. They show up on site and fight alongside you. Each twist names a default merc. If that merc is you, or dead, the next living buddy by trust takes the same lines, so write them to fit anyone. No buddies left: no twists, no rescues, and the game still completes.\n321\t- **Rescue** (the source's signature moment, our staging):\n322\t  1. At 0 hp you drop. Ink closes in, there's a heartbeat, the sound goes underwater. You can crawl slowly and fire your sidearm for about 12 s.\n323\t  2. An available buddy (highest trust and not wounded) runs in, firing, invulnerable until the rescue finishes.\n324\t  3. They kneel over you, syringe in frame, say a line. You're dragged 5–8 m to cover (real camera drag), a pistol is pressed into your hand, and you're up at 2 bars.\n325\t  4. They fight beside you for up to 60 s and then leave. That buddy is now `wounded` until you sleep.\n326\t\n327\t  So two rescues are possible per day at most, and the third time down is real.\n328\t- **Buddy down.** In that fight, or on a twist, a buddy can drop and call for you. Reach them in 60 s and choose, big and first-person: **Syringe** (costs your syringe, `buddySaveChance` 35%, it works or it doesn't) or **Morphine** (mercy, always available). Walking away is also a choice, and they call after you.\n329\t- Buddies are gone for good when dead. The bar stool stays empty and their tab stays open on the wall.\n330\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0178d0d-15a3-488a-af93-32cb0d078d37","uuid":"b602b512-32db-4258-a840-2d0fa1067090","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:04:42.160Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211935__x12d0e8/work/PLAN.md","content":"# PLAN: a first-person open-world mercenary game inspired by Far Cry 2\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- You build the whole game from this plan and your TASK.md, alone. Nobody is available to answer questions: decide and move on. **If this plan and TASK.md disagree, TASK.md wins.**\n- **Title:** yours to pick, by the rule in your TASK.md (a riff, never the source's actual name). Do that first. Nothing here depends on it. `src/data/meta.js` exports `TITLE` and `TAGLINE`, and every screen and tag reads them.\n- **Names:** your TASK.md gives the dev folder's name, the deliverable HTML's name and the exit-interview file's name, so this plan never repeats them. Every path below is relative to the dev folder.\n- **✔ verified** means the planner ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04 with `three@0.180.0`, `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3` and Chromium 153. Don't re-research those. Further research is optional; report whatever you do in Source Information.\n- Everything in this plan is in scope. §15 lists the only things that may shrink, and they shrink in quantity, never in kind. Nothing ships half-built: if something is cut, it's absent, not stubbed.\n- The \"This computer\" section of your TASK.md still applies. §14.1 lists where the planner found it needs a correction.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game on one page\n\n**Pitch.** You are a foreign mercenary in the last dry season of a dying company-state. You came for one clerk and his ledger. You stay because both sides pay on time. The grass burns where the wind takes it, your gun rusts in your hands, the fever comes back on its own schedule, and the friend who dragged you out of the fire yesterday may need you to end it for them tomorrow.\n\n| Decision | Choice | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Genre | Open-world first-person shooter with stealth, survival friction and a branching contract story | That is what makes the source distinct: a place that pushes back |\n| Perspective | First-person, always, in play and in vehicles, with no cutscene camera. NPCs talk to you in the world while you stand, walk or drive. Attract mode alone may use chase and fly-by cameras | \"Stay in your own eyes\" is half of the source's feel |\n| Players | Campaign for 1. A co-op job for 1–4 in AI Hub sessions and on its own (§6) | TASK.md's multiplayer rule |\n| Length | Critical path about 45–60 minutes. All features are seen by about minute 35 | Long enough to show everything |\n\n**Pillars** (when in doubt, these decide):\n\n1. **The land is the weapon.** Wind, dry grass, slope, time of day. The best plan in the game is a flare and a good read of the wind, and the wind can turn.\n2. **Nothing is free, nothing lasts.** Guns rust, trucks break, pills run out, friends die for good. This friction is the drama, so it must always be readable and answerable, never a chore.\n3. **Stay in your own eyes.** Map in your hand, pliers in your arm, papers held out of the truck window. Diegetic first. HUD only for what a couch player can't otherwise read.\n4. **Everybody's money is dirty.** Every contract has a cost you can later see on the map and in the camp.\n5. **Read it from the couch.** At 10 feet, big, few and clear beats realistic and detailed, every time.\n\n**What we keep from the source's design, and what we fix:**\n\n| Keep (as our own version) | Fix |\n|---|---|\n| Spreading grass fire driven by wind | Fire is readable: a cloth strip on your gun and the truck's aerial shows the wind |\n| Guns that wear out and jam; a dealer who sells reliable ones | Condition shows on the gun itself as rust and tape, so a jam is never a surprise you couldn't have seen coming |\n| Recurring fever and pills earned through errands | The first attack never lands mid-fight, and warnings are generous. Later ones can land anywhere |\n| Map and GPS held in your hand, usable on the move and at the wheel | At 10 feet the map can be pulled up to fill the screen |\n| Painful first-person field surgery | Clear, short, interruptible, with several variations |\n| A buddy who drags you out; permanent buddy death; mercy | Rescue is limited per day and telegraphed, so the player can plan around it |\n| Two corrupt factions; buddies offering a better, riskier way to do a job | Consequences stay visible on the map and in the camp |\n| Hidden diamonds found with a locator on the GPS | Same, with light, beep and rumble |\n| Sleep to set the time of day; night is for sneaking | Same, as a watch dial |\n| **Not kept:** guard posts that refill the moment you leave | Cleared posts stay cleared until the next dawn, and only refill if a supply route survives. A real truck brings the men |\n| **Not kept:** every vehicle attacks on sight, always | Faction papers let you bluff through that faction's checkpoints: a real tense scene at the truck window |\n\n**Originality rules (hard).** This is an original game in the source's genre and spirit. Use none of its names, characters, story beats, map, dialogue, music, art or logos: no Jackal, no APR or UFLL, no Pala or Bowa-Seko, no taxi-ride opening, no villain at your bedside. Don't quote its lines or the philosophers it quotes. Our fiction, cast, map and rhetoric (§2, §5) are our own.\n\n**Respect rules (hard).** No real country, continent, ethnic group or language is named or imitated. Locals are people with jobs, jokes and plans, never scenery. Their names are job titles because everyone's are (§2). The game's contempt points at the trade and at the foreigners, you included. No child soldiers, no sexual violence, no torture scenes, no real-world atrocities. Violence is inked and stylised: black ink spatter, charred silhouettes, no gore models.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The world: Concession Fourteen\n\n**Premise.** The country was never a country. It was a mining company's private territory, leased by the square mile: Concession Fourteen. The company folded and left behind its pit, its rail line, its security force and its union. Those last two are now at war, and they still use the company's job titles because names get you killed. *Only foreigners and the dead keep their names here.* The flag is the old company logo (a pick crossed with a pen inside a diamond), painted over by both sides.\n\n**Factions** (both are awful, both are funny, neither is ever \"the good one\"):\n\n- **The Stewardship (\"Stewards\").** Ex-company security holding the rail, the paperwork and the euphemisms. Ochre armbands, khaki. Speak like a quarterly report. Led locally by **the Comptroller**.\n- **The Common Shift (\"the Commons\").** Ex-miners' union holding the diesel, the numbers and the grudges. Red armbands, work clothes. Speak like a shop-floor meeting at gunpoint. Led locally by **the Foreman**.\n- **The Underwriters.** The foreign insurers who have written policies for both sides. Seen only as one agent in a clean shirt on the barge. They hired you.\n\n**Cast** (role-names; each has a fixed voice seed, §12):\n\n| Who | Where | What they are |\n|---|---|---|\n| The Ferryman | The barge | Takes you in. Ferries you between landings. Greets everyone with \"First time?\" |\n| The Widow | Her yard outside Kiln | Arms dealer. Takes diamonds only. Has outlasted four governments |\n| The Comptroller | Old company HQ, Kiln | Stewards' fixer. Never says \"war\" |\n| The Foreman | Union hall, Kiln | Commons' fixer. Third generation in the pit |\n| The Doctor | Dispensary, Kiln | Pays for favours in quinine bark. Runs papers for the people the war made homeless |\n| The Clerk of Second Water | Refugee camp | Keeps the camp's count. The only honest ledger in the game |\n| **The Notary** | Unknown until Act 3 | The company's last clerk. Sells to both sides and writes everything down. Your target. Heard on six dictation tapes. Forty years of pit dust in his lungs |\n\n**The mercs.** Four came in on the same barge. You pick one in the prologue. The other three become your **buddies** in the campaign. In co-op, seats map to them in order. Each has their own first-person hands (skin tone, tattoo or glove) and one small perk.\n\n| Merc | Who | Perk | Buddy role in contracts |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Ines Calloway** | Struck-off field medic. Gentle, dry, terrifying | Syringes heal 25% faster to use | Knows who's hurt and who's hiding |\n| **Tomasz Wren** | Sapper, 50s, deaf in one ear, loud | Repairs and charges 25% faster | Likes it when things fall down |\n| **Dao Renard** | Ex-diamond courier and bush pilot, nervy, always owed money | Locator range +50% | Knows where the money moves |\n| **Sefton Bray** | Oldest merc in the Concession, here for the first war too | Guns wear 25% slower | Knows who owes whom, and why |\n\n**The map.** 2048 m × 2048 m, centred on the origin, y up, x east, z south. Fixed design, the same every game (no random layout). Places have merc nicknames, written in grease pencil on the map. All coordinates are metres. Adjust locally for fit, and keep the table in `src/data/world.js` as the single source of truth.\n\n| Id | Place | x, z | What's there |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `kiln` | **Kiln**, ceasefire town | −60, 110 | 16–20 buildings, market, both fixers, the Doctor, the bar (\"The Last Invoice\"). Weapons auto-holster. Gates at N and E, both flagged |\n| `landing` | **The Landing** | −55, 212 | Barge jetty on the Marrow. Prologue ends here |\n| `yard` | **The Widow's Yard** | 40, 70 | Shipping containers outside the east gate. The gun table |\n| `bridge_road` | **Kiln Bridge** | 130, 60 | Old road bridge east across the Marrow, the only road to the east bank. South-east to the Long Grass, north-east to the Workings |\n| `s1` | Safehouse **Pump House** | −240, −40 | Given in C1 |\n| `s2` | Safehouse **Salt Shed** | 420, 300 | In the Long Grass. Must be taken (C4) |\n| `s3` | Safehouse **Signal Box** | 120, −395 | On the rail line. Optional: clear its guards and it's yours (Salt Shed is the same, through C4) |\n| `gp1` | Post **Junction** | 40, −120 | Crossroads north of Kiln. Commons at start |\n| `gp2` | Post **Grass Road** | 300, 180 | Tank stand, sandbags. Commons at start |\n| `gp3` | Post **Rail Crossing** | 120, −300 | Stewards at start |\n| `gp4` | Post **Depot Road** | −420, −300 | Stewards at start |\n| `gp5` | Post **Workings Gate** | 570, −370 | East bank, where the road and rail meet the pit's fence. Stewards at start |\n| `gp6` | Post **Switchback Foot** | −300, −540 | Foot of the only road up the Escarpment. Nobody's: deserters from both |\n| `gp7` | Post **Low Ford** | −500, 390 | Vehicle ford toward the camp. Commons at start |\n| `gp8` | Post **North Road** | 380, −60 | East bank, on the road from Kiln Bridge to the Workings. Stewards at start |\n| `dump` | **The Fuel Dump** | 560, 520 | Commons diesel tanks, office, in the deepest grass |\n| `railbridge` | **Bad Bridge** | 470, −380 | Steel rail span over the Marrow |\n| `depot` | **West Depot** | −780, −380 | Stewards rail yard, supply base |\n| `workings` | **The Workings** | 760, −430 | East bank. Terraced open pit, about 180 m radius, 40 m deep. Conveyor, sheds, strongroom at the bottom |\n| `mission` | **The Mission** | −420, −820 | Old church and radio mast on the Escarpment. Commons and Stewards both avoid it |\n| `camp` | **Second Water** | −600, 500 | Refugee camp, south bank |\n| `waterhole` | **The Waterhole** | 700, 120 | Herds gather here |\n\n**Terrain.** A pure function `heightAt(x, z)` in `src/sim/terrain.js`, baked to a Float32Array at 2 m (1025 × 1025): low rolling noise (±8 m), then authored features from data.\n\n- **The Marrow:** a spline through (900, −1024), (760, −740), (540, −560), (470, −380), (400, −220), (200, −60), (60, 180), (−120, 290), (−420, 360), (−760, 470), (−1024, 720). Carved 6 m deep, 30–60 m wide. It splits the map: Kiln, the Depot and the Escarpment on the west bank; the Long Grass, the Dump, the Workings and Second Water on the east and south.\n- **The Escarpment:** +80 m plateau north-west of the line from (−1024, −420) to (120, −780), with a single switchback road.\n- **The Workings:** a terraced negative cone.\n- Flat disks under POIs, and 7 m flat corridors along road splines.\n- **Rail line:** from `depot` along z ≈ −380, past Signal Box, over `railbridge`, to the pit's west side.\n- **Roads:** Kiln north gate → Junction → Rail Crossing → Depot Road → Switchback Foot → Mission. Kiln east gate → Yard → Kiln Bridge, then forking to Grass Road → Dump and to North Road → Workings Gate. A west road from Kiln to Low Ford → Second Water.\n- **Biome mask:** tall grass (1.3 m) over the Long Grass quadrant (x > 130, z > 80) and in patches; short grass and bare laterite elsewhere; rock on the Escarpment; green strip along the river.\n- **Scatter (seeded, deterministic):** about 1500 flat-topped acacias, 4000 brush clumps, 800 rocks, 120 termite mounds. Trees and rocks are static colliders.\n\n**Time.** 24 game hours = 36 real minutes by default (tunable). Sleep skips to a chosen time. Dawn, morning, noon, golden hour, dusk and night all look clearly different (§10.3) and change AI vision.\n\n**Wind.** A global angle and speed (0–12 m/s), drifting slowly on seeded noise, with gust events and scripted shifts. Read it from the cloth strip on your weapon and aerial, the grass sway, the smoke lean, and the GPS arrow.\n\n**Weather.** Clear, dust haze, and dry thunder (lightning can start far-off fires). One scripted rain squall in Ending 3's aftermath. Rain puts fires out, and it should feel like an event because the season is dry.\n\n**Ambient life (not optional, it's half the mood).**\n\n- Zebra and antelope herds that flee gunfire and fire.\n- Vultures that circle where bodies are.\n- Dust devils, river mist at dawn, distant artillery flashes at night.\n- Civilians in Kiln and the camp going about their day, and scattering when shots start.\n- Painted road signs at junctions pointing to places (diegetic navigation).\n- Slogans on walls (\"SHIFT'S NOT OVER\", \"STEWARDS KEEP WHAT THEY STEAL\") and company notices (`NOTICE: LEASE TERMS UNCHANGED`).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Loop and pacing\n\n**Core loop:** wake at a safehouse and choose your hour → take a contract (a buddy may call with a better, riskier way) → cross the country with the map in your hand, past posts and patrols → read the ground through the monocular (wind, grass, guards) → do the job your way (burn, shoot, sneak, drive through it) → survive the friction (jam, fever, wound, breakdown) → get paid in diamonds and spend them → see what your work changed → sleep.\n\n**Pacing target for a first play:**\n\n| Minute | Beat | Features first seen |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0–4 | Prologue on the barge | First-person dialogue, merc pick, map, checkpoint and papers |\n| 4–12 | C1 First Wages | Truck, GPS, post fight, forced jam, loot, surgery, shop, locator, safehouse, sleep and save |\n| 12–22 | C2 Dry Grass | Wind, flare, fire, monocular, machete, twist, a buddy rescue if you go down |\n| ~17 | First fever | Warp, pill. Doctor's errand offered |\n| 22–30 | E1 Bark run | Skiff, ferry, camp, consequences |\n| 30–38 | C3 Bad Bridge | Night, charges, explosion physics, train |\n| 38–44 | C4/C5/C6 in player's order | Safehouse capture, convoy, mounted gun, dynamite truck, forged papers |\n| 44–52 | C7 The Workings | Choose a side. Big battle. Mercy |\n| 52–60 | C8 The Notary | Three endings |\n\n**The Director (`src/sim/director.js`) guarantees the first-time moments** so every player sees everything:\n\n| Event | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| First jam | Forced on the first firefight, about the 15th shot, with the C1 starter gun |\n| First breakdown | Scripted engine hit on the road after C1 |\n| First fever | 5 minutes after C1 ends, only when no enemy is alerted within 40 m |\n| First rescue | Guaranteed on the first time you go down, whichever buddy is available |\n| Mercy | C7 (§5) |\n| Fire near you | C2's wind shift is scripted once |\n\nAfter a feature's first use, a one-line tip goes in the notebook.\n\n**Feature list (F-list).** `src/data/features.js` lists every feature id. The sim records each as seen. Tests assert the scripted playthrough covers 100% and attract mode covers at least 80% (§14). Ids:\n\n`fire_spread wind_shift flare_ignite molotov torch_flame rocket explosion_physics tree_burn ember_spot regrow jam jam_clear gun_break rust_visual loot_gun shop_buy armoury_redraw heal_syringe surgery_bullet surgery_finger surgery_shrapnel surgery_burn fever pill collapse rescue buddy_down mercy twist_offer twist_done papers_ok papers_fail post_clear post_regarrison monocular_tag machete_takedown crouch_grass night_stealth map_raise map_focus map_drive gps_waypoint locator_found diamond dictation drive breakdown repair mounted_gun skiff ferry truck_cargo train sleep_time save_load day_night ceasefire_town ragdoll wounded_crawl comrade_drag animals_flee ending_read ending_sell ending_burn coop_revive coop_case`\n\n---\n\n## 4. Systems\n\nAll numbers are defaults. Everything in the Tunables table (§9) reads from `tunables` live. The sim is deterministic: fixed 60 Hz step, seeded RNG streams, no `Math.random` (§13).\n\n### 4.1 Player\n\n- Walk 4.2 m/s, sprint 7.0 (6 s stamina, 4 s regen), crouch 2.2, jump 1.1 m. Eye height 1.62 standing, 1.05 crouched. FOV 75 (setting), 50 when aimed.\n- **Health:** 100, shown as 5 big bars. Damage regenerates only up to the top of the current bar, 4 s after the last hit, at 6 hp/s.\n- **Downed** at 0. See rescue, §4.11. With no rescue available you die: a ledger page (\"Contract void.\") and a reload from the last save.\n- Rapier kinematic character controller (recipe §13.5). Legs visible when you look down. Head bob is subtle and switchable.\n\n### 4.2 Weapons, condition and jams\n\nLoadout: **Sidearm + Long Gun + Heavy**, plus machete and Molotovs. Merc nicknames, no real brand names. Each weapon instance has its own **condition 0–100**.\n\n| Id | Name | Slot | RPM | Dmg | Mag / reserve | Eff. range | Wear/shot | Price | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `pistol` | Service Pistol | Side | 300 | 24 | 12 / 48 | 30 m | 0.10 | start | C1's worn starter |\n| `quiet` | Quiet Pistol | Side | 240 | 22 | 10 / 30 | 22 m | 0.14 | 3 | Heard at 12 m |\n| `flare` | Flare Pistol | Side | 30 | 12 | 1 / 8 | arc 80 m | 0.5 | 2 (free in C2) | Ignites fuel within 2 m of impact; lights the dark |\n| `carbine` | Worker's Carbine | Long | 600 | 28 | 30 / 120 | 60 m | 0.05 | 3 | Commonest loot |\n| `rifle` | Surveyor's Rifle | Long | 45 | 110 | 5 / 20 | 400 m | 0.35 | 6 | Bolt, 4× scope |\n| `pump` | Trench Pump | Long | 65 | 13 × 9 | 6 / 24 | 18 m | 0.25 | 4 | |\n| `belt` | Belt Gun | Heavy | 700 | 26 | 80 / 160 | 70 m | 0.04 | 7 | You walk slowly |\n| `tube` | The Tube | Heavy | 1 shot | 180 splash 6 m | 1 / 4 | 150 m | 1.5 | 8 | Ignites |\n| `torch` | Drip Torch | Heavy | stream | 40 dps | 100 / 200 fuel | 14 m | 0.1/s | 6 | Ignites. Tank can explode if shot |\n\n- **Machete:** 60 dmg, instant silent kill from behind (prompt), no wear.\n- **Molotov:** carry 3, 5 m burn patch, usable from a truck window.\n- **Condition:**\n  - Dealer-fresh 100. Looted 18–55.\n  - Jam chance per shot: `jamK × ((50 − cond) / 50)²` below condition 50, with `jamK` 0.04 by default.\n  - Condition drops 2 when dropped in water or caught in fire.\n- **Jam:**\n  - The gun clicks, a jam sound plays, and the reload button clears it: hands slap and rack, 1.0–1.8 s by weapon class.\n  - Below condition 15 a clear can fail (15%).\n  - At condition 0 the next jam **breaks** the gun: it comes apart in your hands and is gone.\n- **Reading it:** condition drives a `rust` uniform on the view model (noise-thresholded rust blotches, tape wraps below 30, wisp of smoke below 10). No HUD number; the HUD ammo icon only cracks below 25.\n- **Shop model:** buying unlocks a weapon for good. A fresh copy can then be drawn free from any **armoury crate** (the Yard, and safehouses once bought).\n\n**Targets:** enemy guard TTK with carbine about 4 body hits, 1–2 head. Player survives about 8 enemy carbine hits (14 each) from full. Standing in the open at 30 m under 3 guards takes about 1.5 hits/s. Crouched in cover, far less.\n\n### 4.3 Wounds and healing\n\n- **Syringe** (carry 3, pouch upgrade +2, med boxes +1): heals fully in 1.6 s.\n- **Critical** (≤ 1 bar): limp, ink closing in at the screen edges. The heal button now does **field surgery**, free, 2.6–3.5 s, interruptible, chosen by the last damage type. It restores to 2 bars. Four animations: pliers dig a bullet from the forearm (`surgery_bullet`), snap a dislocated finger back (`surgery_finger`), pull shrapnel from the thigh (`surgery_shrapnel`), wrap and bite down on a burn (`surgery_burn`). Each has its own grunt and its own rumble. Keep them short, stylised and memorable.\n\n### 4.4 The fever\n\n- You have malaria-like fever from day one. Never name a real drug beyond \"bark\" (quinine bark).\n- First attack per the Director. Then every 11 ± 2 minutes of play (tunable), anywhere.\n- **Warning, 35 s:**\n  - Warp and desaturate in the post chain.\n  - Ink \"sweat\" running at the screen edges.\n  - Aim sway, ringing in the ears, heartbeat in the rumble.\n  - A small fever icon on the HUD plus the prompt \"Take your dose\".\n- **Last 10 s:** stumbles, flicker blackouts, no sprint.\n- **Pill** (button, 1.8 s, shake the bottle and swallow dry) ends it, with effects fading over 8 s. Start with 2 doses; a bottle is 4; max 8.\n- **No pill: collapse.** Black, a ledger line. You wake 6 hours later at the nearest safehouse, weapon condition −20, one diamond missing. Never a game over.\n- Pills come **only** from the Doctor's errands (E1, E2), and the Doctor gives 2 doses free when you're at zero after a collapse, with a line that makes you feel it. So there is always a way forward.\n\n### 4.5 Fire and wind (the star; build it early)\n\n**Grid:** 512 × 512 cells, 4 m each, typed arrays in `src/sim/fire.js`: `fuel` (0 none, 1 grass, 2 brush, 3 tree, 4 structure), `moist` (0–1), `state` (0 unburnt, 1 burning, 2 burnt), `timer`. Index `iz * 512 + ix`, with `ix = floor((x + 1024) / 4)`. Roads, rock and water have fuel 0. Tick at 10 Hz over a burning set only.\n\n**Spread:** each burning cell tries each of 8 neighbours with chance\n`spreadBase × fuelK × windFactor × slopeK × (1 − moist) × dt`,\nwhere `windFactor = 1 + windK × speedNorm × max(0, dot(windDir, dirToNeighbour))`, or `upwindDamp` when the dot is negative. `slopeK` is greater than 1 uphill.\n\n**Asserted targets** (a test measures these):\n- Dead calm: front ≤ 1 m/s.\n- 10 m/s wind: downwind ≥ 4 m/s (faster than walking, slower than a sprint or a truck).\n- Upwind ≤ 0.3 m/s.\n\n**Burn times:** grass 6–10 s, brush 14 s, tree 40 s. Burning trees throw **embers**, starting spot fires up to 30 m downwind, so a one-cell road can be jumped in strong wind and a river can't.\n\n**Effects:**\n- In or within 2 m of a burning cell: 18 hp/s and a `burning` status for 3 s. NPCs catch, panic-run (spreading it) and fall as charred silhouettes.\n- Barrels and fuel tanks cook off after 3–6 s. Ammo crates pop. Vehicles take heat damage and can blow.\n- NPCs treat burning cells as walls and flee fire fronts; a fire flushes men out of cover.\n- A coarse smoke grid (16 m) blocks AI sight and yours.\n\n**Persistence:**\n- Burnt ground stays black. It's saved (RLE).\n- Green shoots appear after 1 game day, full grass after 3.\n- Burn scars smudge the paper map as charcoal.\n\n**Ignition sources:** flare pistol, Molotov, Drip Torch, The Tube, explosions, burning vehicles, muzzle flash of heavy guns in very dry grass (rare), lightning, Steward fire teams.\n\n### 4.6 Stealth and AI\n\n- **Perception:** vision cone 110°, range 70 m by day, 30 m at night (the player's flashlight or a nearby fire raises it). A detection score fills by distance × light × movement × stance. Crouching in tall grass ×0.35. Smoke blocks. Hearing: carbine 180 m, quiet pistol 12 m, truck 90 m, sprint 12 m.\n- **The HUD shows detection as ink arcs around the crosshair** pointing at whoever is noticing you. Nothing else.\n- **States:** idle/patrol → suspicious (looks, barks) → investigate → alert (calls squad) → combat (cover, flank, suppress, rush) → flee-fire → wounded → dead.\n- **Wounded:** some shot men fall, crawl and beg. A comrade may drag them (`comrade_drag`). They bleed out, or you end it.\n- **Night:** some guards sleep at posts, others carry lamps.\n- **NPC guns jam too** (visible fumbling): an opening for the player.\n- **Implementation:**\n  - A* on a 4 m grid, shared with fire (burning cells are blocked).\n  - Hand-placed cover points per POI in data.\n  - Rapier character controllers within 150 m of a player. Beyond that a cheap 2 Hz \"far\" mode using `heightAt` (✔ 200 controllers plus 200 dynamic bodies run at 93 steps/s in Node here, so far NPCs must not use them).\n  - Line of sight by `castRay` excluding the caster (✔ rays hit your own capsule otherwise).\n- **Archetypes:** guard, sergeant (150 hp, calls flanks), gunner (mounted guns and Belt Gun), fire team (Drip Torch, lights grass on purpose, late game and The Job).\n- **Patrol trucks:** 2–3 per faction on road splines. Chase when alerted, with a gunner firing, and stop 30 m out to dismount. Papers apply to them too (a wave, a slow-down, or a fight).\n- **Death:** hit reaction → ragdoll (§11).\n\n### 4.7 Posts, papers and the ceasefire\n\n- **Post:** 3–5 guards, sandbags, an MG nest or a parked technical, stacked barrels, grass around it you can burn.\n- **Clearing a post** keeps it cleared until the next 06:00, and it only refills if its faction's depot is intact and the road to it isn't cut. Then a **real truck drives in** with 60% of its strength, reaching 100% after two dawns. `dump` burned hurts the Commons; `railbridge` blown hurts the Stewards. Contracts can flip a post's owner.\n- **Papers.** Doing a faction's first contract gives you its papers (a physical card). At that faction's posts, if you approach slowly (< 25 km/h) with the gun lowered, a guard flags you down and walks to your window. You hold the papers out (`papers_ok`). He looks, says something tired, and waves you on. Speed through, or have standing below −10 or contraband in the truck: \"Out of the truck.\" (`papers_fail`), and it's a fight. Other factions' posts shoot on sight. Attacking a faction's post where witnesses survive burns your papers until the next dawn.\n- **Kiln ceasefire** (Second Water too). Weapons holster at the gates, and drawing takes a deliberate long press. Fire a shot in town and both sides' gate guards turn hostile for a game day, the fixers refuse you, and the Widow charges +1.\n\n### 4.8 Vehicles (all Rapier)\n\n- **Pickup** (raycast vehicle, ✔ §13.5), **Technical** (pickup with mounted MG; switch seat with Triangle and the wheel is left free), **Cargo truck** (AI convoys; the dynamite truck in C6), **Skiff** (dynamic body with custom buoyancy and thrust, on the Marrow), **Barge** (scripted, the ferry), **Train** (kinematic on the rail spline, C3).\n- First-person at the wheel with hands on it, working dash, aerial with cloth strip, and the map usable while driving.\n- **Damage:** 300 hp pickup. Below 60% light smoke; below 35% engine cough and −40% top speed; below 15% stall; below 5% burning, blows in 6 s.\n- **Repair:** get out, hold Use at the hood: hood opens, wrench on engine, 4.5 s, restores to 70%. Interruptible. Full repair at safehouses and the Yard.\n- **Ferry:** the Ferryman runs between the Landing, Bridge Pier (near `railbridge`) and Second Water. You sit on the barge, an ink wipe passes time, you're there. A short line each trip. This is the game's fast travel.\n\n### 4.9 Map, GPS, locator, monocular\n\n- **Map (D-pad Up / M):** a physical paper map raised in your left hand, GPS clipped to it. You can walk, run or drive with it up.\n- **Map content:** a CanvasTexture, hand-inked: contours, roads, rail, the Marrow, place names in grease pencil, current contract circled in red, safehouses, posts you've seen with their owner, burn scars redrawn as charcoal, your position as a small red arrow.\n- **Focus (Cross, or left-click, while raised):** the map fills 85% of the screen. Left stick or mouse moves a cursor. R2/L2 or wheel zooms. Cross or left-click sets a waypoint. Square or right-click clears it. Circle or Esc lowers it. Big text only, no pins legend. At the wheel, focusing takes your hands off: the truck coasts and brakes gently until you lower it.\n- **GPS:** big arrow to the waypoint, distance, wind arrow.\n- **Locator:** when a diamond case is within range (120 m, upgradeable), an LED on the GPS blinks with a rising beep and a rumble tick. Faster when closer and facing it, solid green when in line.\n- **Monocular (D-pad Left / B):** zoom 6×. Hold on a guard for 0.6 s to tag them with a small ink triangle shown while in line of sight. Also tags barrels and tanks.\n\n### 4.10 Diamonds, the Widow, safehouses\n\n- **Diamonds** are the only currency. 12 cases are hidden around the map (1–2 each, mostly in places worth seeing: the capsized barge, the water tower at Grass Road, under the rail bridge, the pit's upper terrace).\n- **The gun table.** The Widow's stock lies on a long table in the world. Look at a gun or click it (hover or look moves the focus, with a big card: name, one line, price). Interact to buy. L1/R1 steps through items for pad players who don't want to aim at a table. No grid screens, ever.\n- **Shop upgrades:** Syringe pouch 4, Molotov bandolier 3, Locator range 3, Armoury crate at Salt Shed 2, Armoury crate at Signal Box 2.\n- **Economy target** (printed by `tools/balance.mjs`): contracts pay 3, 6, 7, 3, 6, 7, 10, with twists +3 each and cases about 18. Total about 75 against about 53 in the shop, so a thorough player buys most things but not early.\n- **Safehouses:** a bed, med box, ammo, and an armoury crate where bought. A truck spawns outside if you came on foot. **Bed:** lie down, a large watch fills the view, and you pick Dawn, Noon, Dusk or Midnight. The hands spin and light sweeps through the shutters (ink-wipe time-lapse). Sleeping saves.\n- **Saving:** one save plus one backup in `localStorage`. Autosaves when a contract starts or finishes. \"Continue\" loads the latest. No slot screens.\n\n### 4.11 Buddies, rescue, mercy\n\n- Each buddy has `trust` (starts at 30) and a state: `ok`, `wounded` (until the next sleep) or `dead` (forever). They wait at the bar, and turn up in the world for rescues and twists. Their voices and hands are as in §2.\n- **Twist.** After you accept a contract, a buddy radios or meets you with a better, riskier way: an extra objective, more pay, +10 trust. They show up on site and fight alongside you. Each twist names a default merc. If that merc is you, or dead, the next living buddy by trust takes the same lines, so write them to fit anyone. No buddies left: no twists, no rescues, and the game still completes.\n- **Rescue** (the source's signature moment, our staging):\n  1. At 0 hp you drop. Ink closes in, there's a heartbeat, the sound goes underwater. You can crawl slowly and fire your sidearm for about 12 s.\n  2. An available buddy (highest trust and not wounded) runs in, firing, invulnerable until the rescue finishes.\n  3. They kneel over you, syringe in frame, say a line. You're dragged 5–8 m to cover (real camera drag), a pistol is pressed into your hand, and you're up at 2 bars.\n  4. They fight beside you for up to 60 s and then leave. That buddy is now `wounded` until you sleep.\n\n  So two rescues are possible per day at most, and the third time down is real.\n- **Buddy down.** In that fight, or on a twist, a buddy can drop and call for you. Reach them in 60 s and choose, big and first-person: **Syringe** (costs your syringe, `buddySaveChance` 35%, it works or it doesn't) or **Morphine** (mercy, always available). Walking away is also a choice, and they call after you.\n- Buddies are gone for good when dead. The bar stool stays empty and their tab stays open on the wall.\n","numLines":330,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1189}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GjqtrjTabt2nhqfpoL9cAU","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
