{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012HmpyDuqUoHox7eNMqAWxH","type":"tool_result","content":"669\t**Tunables** (`src/data/tunables.js`: `{id, label, group, min, max, step, def}`). Every slider takes effect immediately in the running sim. Tests check a change takes effect and that reset restores defaults.\n670\t\n671\t| Group | Sliders (default) |\n672\t|---|---|\n673\t| Player | walk 4.2, sprint 7, stamina 6 s, incoming damage ×1, health regen 6/s, syringe count 3 |\n674\t| Guns | wear ×1, jam `jamK` 0.04, looted condition 18–55, player damage ×1 |\n675\t| Enemies | accuracy ×1, damage ×1, vision range 70, night vision 30, reaction 0.4 s, squad size ×1, post refill days 2 |\n676\t| Fire | spread base (calm ≤ 1 m/s), wind effect `windK`, upwind damp, burn time ×1, fire damage 18/s, ember chance, regrow days 3 |\n677\t| Wind & time | wind speed ×1, day length 36 min, time scale 1 |\n678\t| Fever | interval 11 min, warning 35 s, doses per bottle 4 |\n679\t| Buddies | rescue arrival 12 s, rescues per day 2, buddy save chance 35% |\n680\t| Economy | contract pay ×1, shop prices ×1, locator range 120 m |\n681\t| Vehicles | top speed 28 m/s, engine force, damage ×1, repair time 4.5 s |\n682\t\n683\tEvery gameplay variable also keeps a human-readable note on what it does, for designers.\n684\t\n685\t---\n686\t\n687\t## 10. Art direction\n688\t\n689\t### 10.1 The look\n690\t\n691\tThe engine's own toon and node materials are the base (`MeshToonNodeMaterial` with a stepped ramp, TSL post), pushed into **a war correspondent's inked sketchbook**: hard cel bands, black ink outlines from depth and normals, cross-hatching in the shadows, paper grain, sun-bleached ochres. Fire is the one thing that isn't shy: flat, three-tone, ink-outlined flames and fat cartoon-black smoke.\n692\t\n693\t✔ **Target image, already rendered by the planner with the recipes in §13:** dusk gradient sky from burnt orange to blue, a line of three-tone flames (cream, orange, deep red) outlined in black, advancing over a sea of gold grass with a black burnt scar behind, and columns of fat outlined smoke leaning into the sky, with hatching in the shadows. It already looks like a comic panel, and it runs on WebGPU here. Don't drift toward realism: it won't read at 10 feet, and it isn't the brief.\n694\t\n695\t**Nothing looks like the source.** The source was photorealistic. This is ours. Far geometry gets simpler; silhouettes get stronger. Acacias, truck profiles, men in armbands, water towers all read as shapes from the couch.\n696\t\n697\t### 10.2 Palette\n698\t\n699\t| Thing | Hex | | Thing | Hex |\n700\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n701\t| Ink | `#14100C` | | Tall grass tip / base | `#E2C777` / `#A07F3A` |\n702\t| Paper | `#FFF7E6` | | Short grass | `#B79A55` |\n703\t| Earth | `#C48D4E` | | Burnt | `#1B1512` |\n704\t| Laterite road | `#A9552E` | | Regrowth | `#7C9A3E` |\n705\t| Rock | `#8C7B6B` | | Acacia crown / dry leaves | `#59693A` / `#8A8B46` |\n706\t| Rust | `#8A3E22` | | Painted steel | `#6D7A55` |\n707\t| Steward ochre / khaki | `#C9A227` / `#7D7245` | | Commons red / work blue | `#9E2B25` / `#35506B` |\n708\t| Flame core / mid / edge | `#FFE08A` / `#F26A12` / `#B5260F` | | Smoke dark / light | `#2A2522` / `#5B4D43` |\n709\t\n710\tUI: paper on ink, accent `#E0602A`. Seat colours are the hub's: `#e8443a #3b82f6 #22c55e #f5b700`.\n711\t\n712\t### 10.3 Time of day (interpolate between keys)\n713\t\n714\t| Key | Sun colour, intensity | Sky top | Horizon | Fog |\n715\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n716\t| Dawn 06:00 | `#FFC9A0`, 1.6 | `#5F7C9E` | `#F2B98A` | `#E3AE84` |\n717\t| Morning 09:30 | `#FFF0D0`, 3.0 | `#6FA3C4` | `#EBD7AE` | `#E6CF9F` |\n718\t| Noon 12:30 | `#FFF8E8`, 3.6 | `#7FB2CF` | `#F1E3BF` | `#EFE0B5` |\n719\t| Golden 17:30 | `#FFB068`, 2.8 | `#5E7FA0` | `#F0A860` | `#DB9552` |\n720\t| Dusk 19:00 | `#FF8A50`, 1.4 | `#3A4F6E` | `#E07B39` | `#C9733C` |\n721\t| Night 23:30 | `#8FA6D8`, 0.5 | `#0E1726` | `#1E2B45` | `#1A2438` |\n722\t\n723\tNoon: bleached, high contrast, heat shimmer. Night: indigo washes and ink, stars, moon; fire and lamps own the screen.\n724\t\n725\t### 10.4 Fonts\n726\t\n727\tEmbed as base64 woff2 via esbuild's `dataurl` loader, from npm: `@fontsource/bebas-neue` (titles, OFL), `@fontsource/oswald` (UI, OFL), `@fontsource/special-elite` (ledger, tapes, Apache-2.0), `@fontsource/reenie-beanie` (grease pencil on the map, OFL). Latin subset only. `await document.fonts.load()` before drawing any CanvasTexture text.\n728\t\n729\t### 10.5 Asset list (all procedural, in code)\n730\t\n731\t`src/data/prefabs.js` holds data-described prefabs built from a primitive kit (box, cylinder, wedge, corrugated panel, plank stack), with vertex colours, chipped edges, painted decals from CanvasTexture. The engine's own library is fine for generic props: `RoundedBoxGeometry`, `ConvexGeometry` (rocks), `BufferGeometryUtils.mergeGeometries`, `DecalGeometry`, `SkeletonUtils`.\n732\t\n733\t- **Terrain and plants:** terrain chunks; grass tufts (instanced, burnable); acacia (3 shapes), brush, dead tree, rocks, termite mound; reeds.\n734\t- **Town:** shack, tin-roof house, concrete company block, union hall, church and mast, bar with stools and the tab board, market stall, water tower, tank stand, generator, fuel tank, pipe run, conveyor, mine sheds, terraces, strongroom, rail track, signal box, steel bridge (destructible cluster), jetty.\n735\t- **Military:** sandbags, MG nest, watchtower, barrier, shipping container, tent, refugee tents, crates, oil drum (explosive), tire stack.\n736\t- **Signs:** road signs, slogans and posters.\n737\t- **Vehicles:** pickup, technical, cargo truck, skiff, barge, locomotive, wagons, payroll car, bus-sized wreck, patrol boat wreck.\n738\t- **Weapons:** all 9, machete, Molotov, syringe, pliers, wrench, bottle, map, GPS, monocular, recorder, passports, ledger.\n739\t- **Creatures:** zebra, antelope (shared quadruped rig), vulture.\n740\t- **People:** one rig, faction outfits, civilians, the cast (distinct silhouettes: the Widow's headwrap and folding chair, the Comptroller's tie, the Foreman's hard hat, the Doctor's apron, the Notary's cardigan and lamp, four mercs).\n741\t\n742\t**Grass and water notes.** Grass is instanced tufts in 32 m chunks around the camera, dense to about 160 m. Beyond that, a terrain stipple. All blade normals point up for soft cel bands and no noisy edges (✔ that removed the speckle). Water: flat toon bands, a foam line where depth is shallow, and slow noise-driven ripples.\n743\t\n744\t---\n745\t\n746\t## 11. Animation\n747\t\n748\tThree's own `AnimationMixer` is the base; all clips are authored in code as `AnimationClip`s from compact pose tables in `src/anim/`.\n749\t\n750\t- **Humanoid rig** (`rig.js`): about 19 bones (hips, spine, chest, neck, head, shoulders, upper/lower arms, hands, upper/lower legs, feet) with sockets on the hands. Geometry: tapered segments merged into one `SkinnedMesh`, skin weights blended at joints, outfits as extra meshes and vertex colours, faces as bold ink marks.\n751\t- **Clip DSL:** `{ name, dur, loop, keys:[{ t, pose:{ bone:[x,y,z] } }] }`, built into `QuaternionKeyframeTrack`s. Crossfade with `AnimationAction`. Additive layers (`AnimationUtils.makeClipAdditive`, `AdditiveAnimationBlendMode`) for aim, recoil, flinch. Aim pitch is a bone offset after `mixer.update`.\n752\t- **NPC clips (about 30):** idle, smoke, talk, walk, run, crouch walk, aim, fire, reload, clear jam, throw, flinch, fall wounded, crawl, drag comrade, being dragged, panic burn, die front, die back, sit at bar, sleep, wave truck down, inspect papers, kneel, inject, carry case, wave arms at fire.\n753\t- **First-person clips (about 40):**\n754\t  - Idle, fire and reload per weapon class; clear jam per class; gun breaks; machete swings.\n755\t  - Molotov light-and-throw; syringe; the four surgeries; dose from bottle.\n756\t  - Raise and lower map, GPS check; raise and lower monocular; wrench at hood.\n757\t  - Papers held out the window; door and lever; steering left and right, gear; crawl and being dragged; lie down and get up; press Record.\n758\t- **Procedural on top:** weapon sway, breathing, fever tremor, recoil spring, limp.\n759\t- **Hands:** a separate arm rig parented to the camera, scaled small and close so it never clips walls. Camera near 0.05. Per-merc skin and tattoo via vertex colour.\n760\t- **Ragdoll:** at death, swap to about 10 capsules joined with `JointData.spherical` (✔), copy poses back to bones, and freeze after it settles. Keep the newest 60 bodies. Burned corpses turn charred by uniform.\n761\t- **Machines:** wheels (spin, steer, Rapier suspension length), hood and door clips, conveyor, train wheels, mast fall.\n762\t- **Animals:** quadruped rig: graze, walk, gallop, fall. Vultures: glide and flap via vertex shader.\n763\t\n764\t---\n765\t\n766\t## 12. Audio (all code, no files, no samples)\n767\t\n768\t`src/audio/` on Web Audio. Every recipe takes a `BaseAudioContext`, so the same code runs in `OfflineAudioContext` for tests. ✔ Offline rendering works here.\n769\t\n770\t**Buses:** Master → Music, SFX, Voice, UI, with a duck for voices and a low-pass for \"hearing damage\" after near explosions. **Music bus gain = 0.10 by default**, set by the Settings slider. A test reads it on a fresh profile.\n771\t\n772\t**SFX recipes (parameterised, per weapon and surface):**\n773\t- **Guns:** noise transient through band-pass plus a sub thump (sine 120→40 Hz) plus a mechanical click, then a tail through a convolver with a generated IR (plains: long slapback; town: short; pit: boomy).\n774\t- **Distance:** low-pass, and delay by speed of sound for far shots and explosions (`delay = d / 340`).\n775\t- **Jam:** dry click and a hiss of breath. **Explosions:** layered, with debris patter.\n776\t- **Fire:** crackle (filtered noise, random pops), a roar whose level is the count of nearby burning cells, panned to the front's centroid.\n777\t- **Wind and grass:** filtered noise following wind speed. **Birds** by day, **insects** at dusk, frogs by the river, distant artillery.\n778\t- **Vehicles:** detuned saws following RPM, tyre noise by surface, gear clunk, cough when damaged. Skiff outboard. Barge horn. Locomotive.\n779\t- **Body:** footsteps by surface, heartbeat, fever whine with tremolo, syringe, pliers, finger snap.\n780\t- **Locator** beep, map rustle, UI ticks.\n781\t\n782\t**Voices: Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish.** A generator turns each word of the English line into syllables, seeded by the word's hash (so words repeat and it sounds like a language), and speaks them with one voice per speaker: sawtooth through two band-pass formants moving per vowel, noise bursts for consonants, 11–16 syllables/s, pitch contour falling at full stops and rising at question marks. Each cast member has fixed pitch, formant shift and tempo: the Notary low and slow with a dry cough, the Ferryman unhurried, the Comptroller clipped, the Foreman loud, Dao fast, Tomasz too loud and rough. Guards: a family of voices per faction. The Notary's tapes go through a band-limited \"recorder\" filter with hiss.\n783\t\n784\t**Music, composed in code, original.** Pre-render plucks with Karplus-Strong into `AudioBuffer`s (Web Audio's own delay loop can't go short enough for high notes). Palette: thumb-piano ostinato, nylon-string pluck, slide lead by FM glide, bowed drone, frame drum from sine and noise. Don't echo the source's soundtrack.\n785\t\n786\t| Cue | When | Feel |\n787\t|---|---|---|\n788\t| Title | Front end | Slow two-bar ostinato, drone, a slide phrase every 8 bars |\n789\t| Road | Driving | Plucks and hand drum, with long silences |\n790\t| Danger | Alert and combat, by intensity | Drum pulse enters, drone rises |\n791\t| Fever | Fever | Same ostinato detuned and slowing |\n792\t| Fire | Near a big front | Drone and low drum only |\n793\t| Dawn | Waking | Single phrase |\n794\t| Mercy | Mercy scene | Nothing, then one plucked phrase |\n795\t| Ending | Epilogue | Solo pluck, one per ending |\n796\t\n797\tMostly the field is **quiet** and music comes and goes: wind and grass are the score. One vehicle radio cycles music and the Notary's readings.\n798\t\n799\t---\n800\t\n801\t## 13. Technical plan\n802\t\n803\t### 13.1 Stack\n804\t\n805\t`three@0.180.0` with `WebGPURenderer` and TSL ✔; `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3` ✔ (WASM embedded in the JS, so one file works); plain JS modules, DOM UI, Web Audio, Gamepad API; esbuild ✔; Playwright 1.63.0 for tests. `npm install` in the dev folder, and commit `package-lock.json`.\n806\t\n807\t### 13.2 Layout\n808\t\n809\t```\n810\tsrc/\n811\t  main.js         boot, mode routing (standalone, session, test)\n812\t  core/           loop, rng streams, event queue, tunables, save, small fsm helpers\n813\t  data/           meta, world, prefabs, weapons, npcs, contracts, lines, dictations, tunables, shop, attract, features, about.json\n814\t  sim/            index (createSim), terrain, physics, player, weapons, wounds, fever, fire, wind, time, ai/*, posts, vehicles, economy, buddies, factions, contracts, director, job, bot\n815\t  view/           renderer, post, sky, terrain, grass, vegetation, props, water, fire, characters, fpv, vehicles, map, gps, effects\n816\t  anim/           rig, clips (npc, first-person, animal), dsl\n817\t  audio/          engine, sfx, ambience, voice, music\n818\t  ui/             stack and focus, prompts, glyphs, hud, screens/*\n819\t  input/          actions, pad, keyboard, mouse\n820\t  net/            transport, hub, link, protocol, job netcode\n821\t  dev/            devmode, overlays, placement\n822\t  attract/        director\n823\tvendor/session.js  the hub client, unmodified\n824\ttools/            build.mjs, test.mjs, tests/*, hub/ (harness.html, bridge.js, round.js), balance.mjs, validate-data.mjs, shots.mjs\n825\tdocs/             DESIGNER.md, issues-log.md\n826\ttest-output/      what tests leave behind\n827\t```\n828\t\n829\t### 13.3 The one architecture rule: sim and view are separate\n830\t\n831\t- `src/sim` holds all game state and rules. It never touches DOM, canvas or the renderer. It imports only pure code, Rapier and three's math. ✔ `three/webgpu` imports fine in plain Node with no DOM, so `Vector3` and friends can be shared.\n832\t- `const sim = createSim({ scenario, seed, tunables, players })`, `sim.step(inputFrames)` advances 1/60 s, `sim.state` is readable, `sim.drainEvents()` hands view, audio and net their events.\n833\t- View, audio and UI read state and events. They never change state except by input frames and dev commands.\n834\t- This gives you, for free: fast playthrough tests in Node; `AIHub.server` (a sim with nothing else); attract mode (a sim plus a bot); and networked clients. All randomness in the sim goes through named seeded streams.\n835\t- Fixed step with an accumulator, up to 5 catch-up steps per frame. Render interpolates. Under `?test=1` the loop stops and tests call `__game.step(n)`.\n836\t- **Data-driven for designers (TASK.md's \"normal designer tools\").** Everything content-like (places, posts, prefabs, weapons, NPC archetypes, contracts, lines, tunables, attract reel) is plain data in `src/data/`, validated by `tools/validate-data.mjs` and documented in `docs/DESIGNER.md` with short how-tos: add a weapon, a post, a contract, a line, a prefab, a tunable. New content needs no system code.\n837\t\n838\t### 13.4 Rendering recipes (✔ all verified, copy them)\n839\t\n840\t```js\n841\timport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\n842\timport { pass, mrt, output, normalView, uniform, vec2, vec3, vec4, float, Fn, screenUV, screenSize, time, sin, cos,\n843\t  step, smoothstep, length, dot, max, luminance, hash, floor, fract, perspectiveDepthToViewZ, cameraNear, cameraFar,\n844\t  attribute, texture, positionLocal, positionWorld, positionWorldDirection, mix, color, uv, abs, normalize, cross,\n845\t  cameraPosition, mx_noise_float } from 'three/tsl';\n846\t\n847\tconst renderer = new THREE.WebGPURenderer({ antialias: false });   // falls back to WebGL2 by itself\n848\trenderer.setPixelRatio(1); renderer.setSize(innerWidth, innerHeight);\n849\trenderer.shadowMap.enabled = true; renderer.shadowMap.type = THREE.PCFSoftShadowMap;\n850\tdocument.body.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\n851\tawait renderer.init();   // catch: no graphics at all → DOM message, AIHub.fail()\n852\trenderer.onDeviceLost = showGraphicsLostBanner;                    // sim keeps running; reload restores from the save\n853\t\n854\t// Toon ramp: 3 hard bands. Use everywhere.\n855\tconst ramp = new THREE.DataTexture(new Uint8Array([70,70,70,255, 150,150,150,255, 255,255,255,255]), 3, 1, THREE.RGBAFormat);\n856\tramp.minFilter = ramp.magFilter = THREE.NearestFilter; ramp.needsUpdate = true;\n857\tconst mat = new THREE.MeshToonNodeMaterial({ color: 0x8a3e22, gradientMap: ramp });\n858\t\n859\tscene.backgroundNode = mix(color(0xe07b39), color(0x3a4f6e), smoothstep(0.0, 0.45, positionWorldDirection.y.max(0))); // drive with uniforms\n860\tscene.fog = new THREE.Fog(0xc9733c, 70, 460);\n861\t```\n862\t\n863\t**Ink post chain.** `fever`, `inkAmt`, `hatchAmt`, `grainAmt`, `inkWidth` are uniforms. Extend `warp` and the grade with desaturation, vignette, damage ink and the screen-wipe mask.\n864\t\n865\t```js\n866\tconst scenePass = pass(scene, camera);\n867\tscenePass.setMRT(mrt({ output, nrm: normalView }));\n868\tconst colTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('output'), nrmTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('nrm'), depthTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('depth');\n869\tconst warp = Fn(() => {\n870\t  const d = screenUV.sub(0.5);\n871\t  const wob = vec2(sin(screenUV.y.mul(23).add(time.mul(2.3))), cos(screenUV.x.mul(19).add(time.mul(1.7)))).mul(0.006).mul(fever);\n872\t  return screenUV.add(wob).add(d.mul(length(d)).mul(0.05).mul(fever));\n873\t})();\n874\tconst viewDist = (u) => perspectiveDepthToViewZ(depthTex.sample(u).r, cameraNear, cameraFar).negate();\n875\tconst ink = Fn(() => {\n876\t  const px = vec2(inkWidth).div(screenSize);\n877\t  const zC = viewDist(warp), nC = nrmTex.sample(warp).xyz;\n878\t  const isGeo = step(0.5, length(nC));                     // the sky writes no normal\n879\t  let dz = float(0), dn = float(0);\n880\t  for (const [ox, oy] of [[1,0],[-1,0],[0,1],[0,-1]]) {\n881\t    const u = warp.add(vec2(ox, oy).mul(px));\n882\t    dz = dz.add(viewDist(u).sub(zC).abs().div(zC));\n883\t    dn = dn.add(float(1).sub(dot(nrmTex.sample(u).xyz, nC).clamp(0, 1)));\n884\t  }\n885\t  const near = float(1).sub(smoothstep(60, 220, zC));      // normal edges fade with distance\n886\t  return max(smoothstep(0.05, 0.14, dz), smoothstep(0.5, 1.1, dn).mul(isGeo).mul(near));\n887\t})();\n888\tconst graded = Fn(() => {\n889\t  const c = colTex.sample(warp).rgb, lum = luminance(c), p = screenUV.mul(screenSize);\n890\t  const h1 = step(fract(p.x.add(p.y).mul(0.125)), 0.25).mul(float(1).sub(smoothstep(0.16, 0.30, lum)));\n891\t  const h2 = step(fract(p.x.sub(p.y).mul(0.125)), 0.25).mul(float(1).sub(smoothstep(0.05, 0.16, lum)));\n892\t  const hatch = max(h1, h2).mul(hatchAmt).mul(0.45).mul(step(0.5, length(nrmTex.sample(warp).xyz)));\n893\t  const g = floor(p.mul(0.5));\n894\t  const grain = hash(g.x.add(g.y.mul(1971)).add(floor(time.mul(8)).mul(313))).sub(0.5).mul(grainAmt);\n895\t  return vec4(c.mul(float(1).sub(hatch)).mul(float(1).sub(ink.mul(inkAmt).mul(0.94))).mul(vec3(1.0, 0.97, 0.9)).add(grain), 1);\n896\t})();\n897\tconst post = new THREE.PostProcessing(renderer);\n898\tpost.outputNode = graded;\n899\t// each frame: post.render();\n900\t```\n901\t\n902\t**Burnable, swaying grass.** A DataTexture `burnTex` (512², RGBA8: R burnt, G burning, B regrow) mirrors the fire grid. Terrain, grass and trees all sample it.\n903\t\n904\t```js\n905\t// Geometry: a thin ConeGeometry tuft (translate up by half its height, set every normal to 0,1,0), wrapped in an\n906\t// InstancedBufferGeometry: copy index, position, normal and uv; setAttribute('instOffset', new\n907\t// THREE.InstancedBufferAttribute(offsets, 3)); set instanceCount; mesh.frustumCulled = false.\n908\t// windDir = uniform(new THREE.Vector2(1, 0.3)), windStr = uniform(0.5). burnTex rows run with z, as the fire grid does.\n909\tconst toBurnUV = (xz) => xz.div(2048).add(0.5);\n910\tconst off = attribute('instOffset', 'vec3');\n911\tconst h = positionLocal.y.div(1.3);\n912\tconst b = texture(burnTex, toBurnUV(off.xz));\n913\tconst sway = sin(time.mul(2.1).add(off.x.mul(0.31)).add(off.z.mul(0.27))).mul(windStr).mul(h).mul(h).mul(0.5);\n914\tgrassMat.positionNode = positionLocal.mul(vec3(1, float(1).sub(b.r.mul(0.85)), 1))\n915\t  .add(vec3(windDir.x.mul(sway), 0, windDir.y.mul(sway))).add(off);\n916\tgrassMat.colorNode = mix(mix(color(0xa07f3a), color(0xe2c777), h), color(0x14100c), b.r);\n917\tterrainMat.colorNode = mix(color(0xc48d4e), color(0x1b1512), texture(burnTex, toBurnUV(positionWorld.xz)).r);\n918\t```\n919\t\n920\t**Flames and smoke:** instanced, y-locked billboard cards, alpha-cut, stepped tones. Because they write depth, the ink pass outlines them.\n921\t\n922\t```js\n923\t// Geometry: PlaneGeometry(1, 1) translated up by 0.5, wrapped in an InstancedBufferGeometry as above.\n924\tconst inst = attribute('fireInst', 'vec4');            // xyz position, w size\n925\tfireMat = new THREE.MeshBasicNodeMaterial({ side: THREE.DoubleSide });\n926\tfireMat.positionNode = Fn(() => {\n927\t  const toCam = normalize(cameraPosition.sub(inst.xyz).mul(vec3(1, 0, 1)));\n928\t  const right = cross(vec3(0, 1, 0), toCam);\n929\t  return inst.xyz.add(right.mul(positionLocal.x.mul(inst.w))).add(vec3(0, positionLocal.y.mul(inst.w), 0));\n930\t})();\n931\tconst vuv = uv();\n932\tconst flame = Fn(() => {\n933\t  const seed = inst.x.mul(3.1).add(inst.z.mul(1.7));\n934\t  const n = mx_noise_float(vec3(vuv.x.mul(3).add(seed), vuv.y.mul(2.2).sub(time.mul(1.8)), seed)).mul(0.5).add(0.5);\n935\t  return float(1).sub(abs(vuv.x.sub(0.5)).mul(2)).mul(float(1).sub(vuv.y)).mul(1.6).mul(n.add(0.35));\n936\t})();\n937\tfireMat.opacityNode = flame; fireMat.alphaTestNode = float(0.18);\n938\tfireMat.colorNode = mix(color(0xb5260f), mix(color(0xf26a12), color(0xffe08a), step(0.55, flame)), step(0.32, flame)).mul(1.5);\n939\tfireMat.fog = false;\n940\t// Smoke: the same card with the same billboard (w grows with height, stacked in rising columns):\n941\tconst puff = Fn(() => {\n942\t  const seed = inst.x.mul(0.7).add(inst.y.mul(0.4));\n943\t  const n = mx_noise_float(vec3(vuv.mul(2.6).add(seed), time.mul(0.15))).mul(0.5).add(0.5);\n944\t  return float(1).sub(length(vuv.sub(vec2(0.5, 0.5))).mul(2)).add(n.mul(0.45).sub(0.12));\n945\t})();\n946\tsmokeMat.opacityNode = puff; smokeMat.alphaTestNode = float(0.12);\n947\tsmokeMat.colorNode = mix(color(0x2a2522), color(0x5b4d43), step(0.42, puff));\n948\t```\n949\t\n950\tPool about 3000 flame cards on the nearest burning cells, about 600 smoke cards in rising columns (rounded, two-tone, ink-outlined), and embers.\n951\t\n952\t**Pitfalls found the hard way (✔ each one bit the planner):**\n953\t\n954\t- `transformedNormalView` is deprecated in r180. Use `normalView`.\n955\t- TSL `hash()` takes **one float**. Give it a vec2 and you get vertical stripes.\n956\t- `smoothstep(a, b, x)` needs `a < b`. For a falling edge, use `1 − smoothstep`.\n957\t- A loose `.discard()` that isn't part of the material's graph does nothing. Use `opacityNode` + `alphaTestNode`.\n958\t- The sky writes a zero normal: guard normal-edge and hatch tests with `step(0.5, length(n))`, or the sky turns to ink.\n959\t- Drive everything from `uniform()`s. Changing a node graph recompiles.\n960\t- **Keep the light count constant** (pool of point lights at intensity 0 when unused; changing the count recompiles materials).\n961\t- Shadows: one directional light with a 4096 map following the camera, snapped to texels. `three/addons/csm/CSMShadowNode.js` exists if you want more.\n962\t- Toon materials and `InstancedMesh`, skinning, node `positionNode` all work together ✔.\n963\t- Don't use compute nodes for anything that matters: three's WebGL2 fallback must keep working. ✔ With no WebGPU (the hub's default test browser) it draws the whole ink chain with no code change.\n964\t\n965\tPerformance is out of scope, as TASK.md says: add all the detail, lights and effects the scene wants. The only rule here is §6.2 (don't starve the protocol) and don't exhaust memory (20 GB shared, §14.1).\n966\t\n967\t### 13.5 Rapier recipes (✔ all verified)\n968\t\n969\t```js\n970\timport RAPIER from '@dimforge/rapier3d-compat';\n971\tawait RAPIER.init();               // console.warn \"using deprecated parameters\": harmless, it's Rapier's own\n972\tconst world = new RAPIER.World({ x: 0, y: -9.81, z: 0 }); world.timestep = 1 / 60;\n973\t\n974\t// Heightfield: column-major, x along cols, z along rows, centred on the origin. nrows, ncols are cell counts.\n975\tconst n = 1024, size = 2048, heights = new Float32Array((n + 1) * (n + 1));\n976\tfor (let col = 0; col <= n; col++) for (let row = 0; row <= n; row++)\n977\t  heights[col * (n + 1) + row] = heightAt((col / n - 0.5) * size, (row / n - 0.5) * size);\n978\tworld.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.heightfield(n, n, heights, { x: size, y: 1, z: size }),\n979\t  world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.fixed()));\n980\t\n981\t// Rays: hit.timeOfImpact. They hit the caster's own capsule unless excluded.\n982\tworld.castRay(new RAPIER.Ray(origin, dir), maxToi, true, undefined, undefined, myCollider);\n983\t\n984\t// Character controller\n985\tconst cc = world.createCharacterController(0.02);\n986\tcc.setUp({ x: 0, y: 1, z: 0 }); cc.enableAutostep(0.4, 0.2, true); cc.enableSnapToGround(0.5);\n987\tcc.setMaxSlopeClimbAngle(50 * Math.PI / 180); cc.setMinSlopeSlideAngle(40 * Math.PI / 180);\n988\tcc.setApplyImpulsesToDynamicBodies(true);\n989\tconst body = world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.kinematicPositionBased().setTranslation(x, y, z));\n990\tconst col = world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.capsule(0.6, 0.35), body);\n991\t// each step\n992\tcc.computeColliderMovement(col, desired); const m = cc.computedMovement(), grounded = cc.computedGrounded();\n993\tconst p = body.translation(); body.setNextKinematicTranslation({ x: p.x + m.x, y: p.y + m.y, z: p.z + m.z });\n994\t\n995\t// Raycast vehicle: set forward to +z as below. ✔ 2500 N on each rear wheel reaches about 16 m/s in 5 s\n996\tconst chassis = world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic().setTranslation(x, y, z).setCanSleep(false));\n997\tconst chassisCol = world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(1.0, 0.45, 2.2).setMass(1400), chassis);\n998\tconst veh = world.createVehicleController(chassis);\n999\tveh.indexUpAxis = 1; veh.setIndexForwardAxis = 2;      // yes, that one is a setter named setIndexForwardAxis\n1000\tfor (const w of [[-0.9,-0.3,1.5],[0.9,-0.3,1.5],[-0.9,-0.3,-1.5],[0.9,-0.3,-1.5]])\n1001\t  veh.addWheel({ x:w[0], y:w[1], z:w[2] }, { x:0, y:-1, z:0 }, { x:-1, y:0, z:0 }, 0.45, 0.42);\n1002\tfor (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) { veh.setWheelSuspensionStiffness(i, 28); veh.setWheelMaxSuspensionTravel(i, 0.4);\n1003\t  veh.setWheelFrictionSlip(i, 2.2); veh.setWheelSuspensionCompression(i, 3); veh.setWheelSuspensionRelaxation(i, 4); }\n1004\t// each step, before world.step(): setWheelEngineForce(rear), setWheelSteering(front), setWheelBrake, then\n1005\tveh.updateVehicle(1 / 60, RAPIER.QueryFilterFlags.EXCLUDE_DYNAMIC, undefined, (c) => c.handle !== chassisCol.handle);\n1006\t// for wheels in view: wheelRotation(i), wheelSteering(i), wheelSuspensionLength(i), wheelIsInContact(i)\n1007\t\n1008\t// Ragdoll joint\n1009\tworld.createImpulseJoint(RAPIER.JointData.spherical({ x:0, y:-0.3, z:0 }, { x:0, y:0.3, z:0 }), bodyA, bodyB, true);\n1010\t\n1011\t// Physics overlay: skip the terrain, or you get 3 million vertices\n1012\tworld.debugRender(undefined, (c) => c.handle !== terrainHandle);\n1013\t```\n1014\t\n1015\tSnapshots replay exactly (✔ `takeSnapshot` / `World.restoreSnapshot`), but game saves are your own JSON; rebuild physics on load.\n1016\t\n1017\t### 13.6 Single-file build (✔)\n1018\t\n1019\t`tools/build.mjs`:\n1020\t\n1021\t1. Run `esbuild src/main.js --bundle --format=esm --target=es2022 --minify --loader:.woff2=dataurl --outfile=build/bundle.js`.\n1022\t2. Fail if the bundle contains `</script`.\n1023\t3. Assemble with **string concatenation, not `.replace()`** (bundles contain `$&`):\n1024\t\n1025\t```html\n1026\t<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n1027\t<script>…vendor/session.js exactly…</script>\n1028\t<title>…</title> …metas… <style>…</style>\n1029\t</head><body><script type=\"module\">…bundle…</script></body></html>\n1030\t```\n1031\t\n1032\t4. Write it to the deliverable path TASK.md names (root, beside the dev folder). TASK.md's folder and file names match, so derive it from the dev folder's own name.\n1033\t\n1034\t✔ With three and Rapier inlined, this came to 3.1 MB, opens on `file://`, makes no network requests, and passes the harness when served. Inline everything; no CDN.\n1035\t\n1036\t---\n1037\t\n1038\t## 14. Testing\n1039\t\n1040\tTASK.md's rule: one command, printing only failures and a one-line count. Rerun only after changing what it covers. Use scripts first, screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. That command is **`node tools/test.mjs`**, with `--sim`, `--ui`, `--hub` to run one tier.\n1041\t\n1042\t### 14.1 This computer: corrections and facts (✔)\n1043\t\n1044\t- **TASK.md's three WebGPU flags aren't enough here.** They give an adapter, but the device is destroyed on the first canvas frame, and everything renders blank with no JS error. **Use the hub's flags. These give real pixels, and a plain Playwright screenshot of the WebGPU canvas works:**\n1045\t  ```\n1046\t  --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader\n1047\t  ```\n1048\t- Use `chromium.launch({ headless: true, args })` (the headless shell). The full browser (`channel: 'chromium'`, or headed) aborts here with \"Socket path too long\" because of the long `TMPDIR`. Headed under xvfb got no WebGPU adapter anyway.\n1049\t- With **no flags** Chromium has no adapter, and three falls back to WebGL2 and still draws (✔). That's how the hub's own check opens games by default, so test that path too: `backend: webgl2`, no errors, screen drawn. WebKit with the iPhone profile also falls back and runs (✔ `backend: webgl2`, `coarse` pointer). Chromium with a Pixel profile has WebGPU and a coarse pointer (✔). Firefox here reported no WebGL2 in its headless run; a graceful message is the only requirement there.\n1050\t- Software WebGPU runs at about 0.3–1.5 fps at 1280 × 720 with 60–150k instances. So **never** assert on real-time behaviour through the renderer. Use `__game.step()`, and small viewports unless checking 10-foot layout at 1920 × 1080.\n1051\t- Pointer lock is granted after a click in headless, but `movementX/Y` then stay 0 (✔). Test look through the virtual input or synthetic events.\n1052\t- `OfflineAudioContext` renders correctly (✔ gain 0.1 peaks at 0.0999). `AudioContext` starts `running` here, but real browsers need a gesture.\n1053\t- Everything you make stays in your dev folder, and you read nothing outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside yours.\n1054\t- Run one browser at a time. A 4-copy harness run is one browser but heavy. \"Target closed\" means out of memory; lower the load, don't retry blindly.\n1055\t- Serve with Node's `http.createServer().listen(0, '127.0.0.1')` inside `tools/test.mjs` (no extra process, no port clash) or `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, and check the page's title before trusting a result. Stop only what you started.\n1056\t\n1057\t### 14.2 Test hooks\n1058\t\n1059\t`window.__game` (always present, read-only except under `?test=1`):\n1060\t\n1061\t- `state()`: compact JSON: screen, focused id, prompts' glyph set, player, weapons with condition, jam state, health, doses, contract step, time, wind, fire counts, attract status, tunables, bus gains, backend, net stats, feature coverage.\n1062\t- `step(n)`, `input(frame)`, `dev(cmd, ...args)` (the same commands as the dev menu), and `renderRecipe(name)`, which returns peak, RMS and duration through `OfflineAudioContext`.\n1063\t\n1064\tPad stub via `addInitScript`, replacing `navigator.getGamepads` with a standard-mapped pad whose buttons and axes tests set, and counting `playEffect` calls.\n1065\t\n1066\t### 14.3 Tiers\n1067\t\n1068\t**`--sim` (Node, no browser, fast). Most of the suite lives here.**\n1069\t\n1070\t- Fire: front speeds at calm, downwind and upwind (§4.5); roads and water stop it unless embers jump; burnt cells persist through save and load; regrowth timing.\n1071\t- Guns: jam rate curve; wear; break at 0; looted range; every weapon fires, reloads and clears.\n1072\t- Fever: schedule, warning, pill, collapse and wake; no first attack near alerted enemies; doses always obtainable.\n1073\t- Heal: bars, regen ceiling, surgery by damage type. Rescue chain and per-day limit; buddy death is permanent; mercy.\n1074\t- AI: perception factors (day, night, grass, smoke), fire avoidance, wounded and drag; posts stay clear until dawn and refill by a real truck only with supply.\n1075\t- Vehicles: drives 100 m; damage stages; stall; repair. Skiff floats and moves. Papers pass and fail.\n1076\t- Economy: `tools/validate-data.mjs` passes; no unreachable contract, line id or prefab.\n1077\t- Determinism: two runs, same seed and inputs, same state hash after 5 sim-minutes. No NaN positions, no NPC stuck over 30 s.\n1078\t- **Scripted whole-game playthrough (`sim/bot.js`):** a bot playing through **input frames only** from the barge through every contract, twists on, to each of the three endings (re-loading the save before the choice). God mode and the dev Trigger commands are allowed, to force rare events on schedule. No other state edits. Assert world-state consequences at each step. Also a shorter run of The Job with bots in four seats. **Together these must reach F-list coverage of 100%.**\n1079\t- `tools/balance.mjs` (not gated): mortal-bot survival, TTKs, jam frequency, money curve. Read it when tuning.\n1080\t\n1081\t**`--ui` (Playwright, Chromium with the flags above, `file://`).**\n1082\t\n1083\t- Boots with no page errors or console errors (the Rapier `console.warn` is fine). `backend: webgpu`, then again with no flags: `backend: webgl2`.\n1084\t- Every screen reachable with the pad stub, then with keyboard, then with mouse. Focus visible and never lost. Back always works. Mouse hover moves focus. Click activates.\n1085\t- Prompt glyphs follow last input. Xbox option swaps to A B X Y.\n1086\t- 10-foot audit at 1920 × 1080 on every screen: font sizes, safe area, ≤ 7 focusables.\n1087\t- Touch-only (Chromium Pixel profile, tap with `page.touchscreen`): message, Settings button works, no other menu, attract runs. Also WebKit iPhone profile.\n1088\t- Attract: starts between 10 and 11 s idle on title; any of key, mouse, button or stick exits with focus intact; loops past a full cycle; coverage ≥ 80%.\n1089\t- Dev mode: reachable from Pause; every page opens; a slider changes behaviour live (for example, fire spread); reset restores; hidden in sessions.\n1090\t- Audio: fresh profile music bus = 0.10. Every SFX, voice and music recipe renders non-silent, finite, in range. Voice duration tracks text length. The same line gives the same audio. No `<audio>`, no audio data URIs, no media fetches in the bundle.\n1091\t- No network requests from the page at all. No `TODO|FIXME|placeholder|stub` in `src/`.\n1092\t- Pixels (`page.screenshot` at low res, compare numbers, don't eyeball): ink present (dark-pixel ratio in a range), sky not black, the fire scene has flame colours, fever changes the picture.\n1093\t- About & Credits shows every Source Information field and all feedback answers. The exit-interview JSON parses and has every required key, and no value is empty.\n1094\t\n1095\t**`--hub` (served harness; run exactly what the hub runs, from its `scripts/hub-check.mjs`).** One harness URL per case, against the built HTML copied next to `tools/hub/*` in `test-output/site/`. A case passes when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true.\n1096\t\n1097\t```\n1098\tharness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&play=8000\n1099\tharness.html?game=GAME.html&players=3&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&play=8000\n1100\tharness.html?game=GAME.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&play=8000\n1101\tharness.html?game=GAME.html&players=3&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave&play=8000\n1102\tharness.html?game=GAME.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave&play=8000\n1103\tharness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=server&play=8000\n1104\t```\n1105\t\n1106\tRun these with the flags from §14.1, and the 4-player case once more with no flags: that is the hub's default browser, where every copy is on WebGL2 in software. Give each page up to 300 s, as the hub does. HARNESS_CLAIM\n1107\t\n1108\tThen:\n1109\t\n1110\t- **The game on its own**, as the hub checks it: open it with no flags, press Enter, Space, Enter, Space, and assert no page errors, `AIHub.active === false`, `AIHub.seeded` not true, and a screen that draws (DOM title text alone is enough to pass while the scene loads).\n1111\t- **Co-op for real:** two pages in a harness round, scripted, where p1 ignites, p2 sees it; p2 goes down, p1 revives; case reaches the barge; `finish` has `won` and correct scores.\n1112\t- **Net budget:** `send()` never returns `false`. Copies stay under 20 messages/s. `s` stays ≤ 1200 bytes.\n1113\t- **Link play:** two pages, host and join, 10 s of play, traffic both ways, results shown.\n1114\t\n1115\tScreenshots for eyes only: a short `node tools/shots.mjs` set at 1920 × 1080 (each time of day, fire, night, menus, map focus, surgery) into `test-output/`. Use it for art passes, not as a test.\n1116\t\n1117\t---\n1118\t\n1119\t## 15. Build order and scale\n1120\t\n1121\tEach milestone ends with its tests passing. Fire and the look come early, because they are the game. Do the title first.\n1122\t\n1123\t| # | Milestone | Done when |\n1124\t|---|---|---|\n1125\t| 0 | Title, scaffold, recipes §13 proven in your folder, `tools/build.mjs`, `tools/test.mjs` (real from the start) | Bundle opens on `file://`, WebGPU backend, ink chain draws, one sim test and one harness run pass |\n1126\t| 1 | Sim core, terrain, physics, player, world data, chunks, sky and time, grass, trees | Walk the whole map in ink-shaded savanna, dawn to night |\n1127\t| 2 | **Fire and wind**, burn texture, flames, smoke, cloth strip | Speed tests pass; a lit field looks like the target image |\n1128\t| 3 | Weapons, condition, jams, first-person hands and clips, machete, Molotov; NPC rig and clips; AI; posts; ragdolls | Clear a post, get jammed, see a man crawl |\n1129\t| 4 | Health, surgery, fever, buddies, rescue, mercy | All trigger from dev commands |\n1130\t| 5 | Pickup, technical, truck, skiff; damage and repair; map, GPS, locator, monocular | Drive with the map up; find a case |\n1131\t| 6 | Economy, gun table, safehouses, bed, save and load, papers, ceasefire | Buy, sleep, reload a save |\n1132\t| 7 | UI system, glyphs, title, pause, settings, HUD, notebook, dev mode, tunables | All UI tests pass on pad, keyboard, mouse |\n1133\t| 8 | Contract runner and **all content**, Director, cast, the writing, the world's people and animals | Whole-game bot playthrough passes, all 3 endings |\n1134\t| 9 | Audio: buses, SFX, voices, music | Audio tests pass, music at 10% |\n1135\t| 10 | Attract reel; touch-only flow | Attract tests pass |\n1136\t| 11 | The Job, netcode, card, hub boot, link play | All `--hub` tests pass |\n1137\t| 12 | Polish from screenshots at 1920 × 1080; about.json; exit-interview; final build; cleanup | §16 |\n1138\t\n1139\t**Scale knobs.** If you run short, cut *quantity* from this list, and remove the cut thing entirely:\n1140\t\n1141\t| Item | Plan | Floor |\n1142\t|---|---|---|\n1143\t| Contracts | C1–C8, E1, E2 | C1, C2, C3, E1, C4, one of C5/C6, C7, C8 |\n1144\t| Weapons | 9 | 6 (`pistol`, `flare`, `carbine`, one of `rifle`/`pump`, one of `belt`/`tube`, `torch`) |\n1145\t| Posts | 8 | 4 |\n1146\t| Cases / tapes | 12 / 6 | 6 / 4 |\n1147\t| Attract vignettes | 14 | 10 (≥ 80% of what remains of the F-list) |\n1148\t| Kiln buildings | 16–20 | 8 |\n1149\t| Scatter counts, NPC variety, music cues | As listed | Half |\n1150\t\n1151\t**Never cut:** fire and wind, jams and rust, surgery, fever, rescue and mercy, map, GPS and locator, truck repair, a boat, papers, sleep and day/night, a buddy twist, all three endings, The Job in hub sessions, dev mode with sliders, attract, 10-foot UI with glyph swap, About & Credits.\n1152\t\n1153\t---\n1154\t\n1155\t## 16. Done checklist\n1156\t\n1157\tBefore you finish:\n1158\t\n1159\t1. `node tools/test.mjs` passes: one line, zero failures. Do a last read of `docs/issues-log.md`.\n1160\t2. The deliverable HTML is in the root as TASK.md names it, with the session client first and unmodified, works on `file://` and when served, and makes no network requests.\n1161\t3. No TODOs, placeholders or stubs. Everything in the menus works. Every feature reachable.\n1162\t4. A fresh profile really has music at 10%.\n1163\t5. `src/data/about.json` is final: Source Information (date, model and environment copied from your TASK.md; `Plan` is this file's name; `Usage` reads \"added on import\"), and honest answers to every Testing, Engine and Prompt question. About & Credits shows them. The exit-interview JSON sits beside the HTML and parses. **Also put all of it in your final chat reply, as TASK.md requires.**\n1164\t6. `docs/DESIGNER.md` is true.\n1165\t7. Delete `node_modules/` and `test-output/` if they aren't needed to review the run (`npm ci` restores them). Stop any process you started. Leave nothing in `/tmp`.\n1166\t\n1167\t### Where each TASK.md item is covered\n1168\t\n1169\t| TASK.md | Here |\n1170\t|---|---|\n1171\t| Source, Goal, Scope (original, feature-complete, vertical slice) | §1, §2, §3, §5, §13.3 |\n1172\t| Genre, Perspective | §1 |\n1173\t| Game Title | §0 (builder's) |\n1174\t| Research | §0 |\n1175\t| Package (single HTML), Working Environment (dev folder, no outside reads, ports, PIDs) | §13.6, §14.1 |\n1176\t| Tech Stack (r180, WebGPU, ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad) | §13, §7.2, §12 |\n1177\t| Art Direction | §10 |\n1178\t| Animation | §11 |\n1179\t| Audio (code only, gibberish voices, music at 10%) | §12 |\n1180\t| Online, Multiplayer (hub and on its own) | §6 |\n1181\t| Performance | §13.4 |\n1182\t| Testing (one command, whole-game playthrough, no audit agents) | §14 |\n1183\t| Dev mode, sliders, reset | §9 |\n1184\t| Attract Mode | §8 |\n1185\t| Narrative tone | §5.1 |\n1186\t| UI & UX, Touch-only | §7 |\n1187\t| Done | §15, §16 |\n1188\t| Source Information, Testing/Engine/Prompt Feedback, exit interview, `aiHub` block | §7.5, §16 |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0178d0d-15a3-488a-af93-32cb0d078d37","uuid":"8aa425a7-f94c-44cf-b8b4-9328124f3771","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:05:02.696Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211935__x12d0e8/work/PLAN.md","content":"**Tunables** (`src/data/tunables.js`: `{id, label, group, min, max, step, def}`). Every slider takes effect immediately in the running sim. Tests check a change takes effect and that reset restores defaults.\n\n| Group | Sliders (default) |\n|---|---|\n| Player | walk 4.2, sprint 7, stamina 6 s, incoming damage ×1, health regen 6/s, syringe count 3 |\n| Guns | wear ×1, jam `jamK` 0.04, looted condition 18–55, player damage ×1 |\n| Enemies | accuracy ×1, damage ×1, vision range 70, night vision 30, reaction 0.4 s, squad size ×1, post refill days 2 |\n| Fire | spread base (calm ≤ 1 m/s), wind effect `windK`, upwind damp, burn time ×1, fire damage 18/s, ember chance, regrow days 3 |\n| Wind & time | wind speed ×1, day length 36 min, time scale 1 |\n| Fever | interval 11 min, warning 35 s, doses per bottle 4 |\n| Buddies | rescue arrival 12 s, rescues per day 2, buddy save chance 35% |\n| Economy | contract pay ×1, shop prices ×1, locator range 120 m |\n| Vehicles | top speed 28 m/s, engine force, damage ×1, repair time 4.5 s |\n\nEvery gameplay variable also keeps a human-readable note on what it does, for designers.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Art direction\n\n### 10.1 The look\n\nThe engine's own toon and node materials are the base (`MeshToonNodeMaterial` with a stepped ramp, TSL post), pushed into **a war correspondent's inked sketchbook**: hard cel bands, black ink outlines from depth and normals, cross-hatching in the shadows, paper grain, sun-bleached ochres. Fire is the one thing that isn't shy: flat, three-tone, ink-outlined flames and fat cartoon-black smoke.\n\n✔ **Target image, already rendered by the planner with the recipes in §13:** dusk gradient sky from burnt orange to blue, a line of three-tone flames (cream, orange, deep red) outlined in black, advancing over a sea of gold grass with a black burnt scar behind, and columns of fat outlined smoke leaning into the sky, with hatching in the shadows. It already looks like a comic panel, and it runs on WebGPU here. Don't drift toward realism: it won't read at 10 feet, and it isn't the brief.\n\n**Nothing looks like the source.** The source was photorealistic. This is ours. Far geometry gets simpler; silhouettes get stronger. Acacias, truck profiles, men in armbands, water towers all read as shapes from the couch.\n\n### 10.2 Palette\n\n| Thing | Hex | | Thing | Hex |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ink | `#14100C` | | Tall grass tip / base | `#E2C777` / `#A07F3A` |\n| Paper | `#FFF7E6` | | Short grass | `#B79A55` |\n| Earth | `#C48D4E` | | Burnt | `#1B1512` |\n| Laterite road | `#A9552E` | | Regrowth | `#7C9A3E` |\n| Rock | `#8C7B6B` | | Acacia crown / dry leaves | `#59693A` / `#8A8B46` |\n| Rust | `#8A3E22` | | Painted steel | `#6D7A55` |\n| Steward ochre / khaki | `#C9A227` / `#7D7245` | | Commons red / work blue | `#9E2B25` / `#35506B` |\n| Flame core / mid / edge | `#FFE08A` / `#F26A12` / `#B5260F` | | Smoke dark / light | `#2A2522` / `#5B4D43` |\n\nUI: paper on ink, accent `#E0602A`. Seat colours are the hub's: `#e8443a #3b82f6 #22c55e #f5b700`.\n\n### 10.3 Time of day (interpolate between keys)\n\n| Key | Sun colour, intensity | Sky top | Horizon | Fog |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Dawn 06:00 | `#FFC9A0`, 1.6 | `#5F7C9E` | `#F2B98A` | `#E3AE84` |\n| Morning 09:30 | `#FFF0D0`, 3.0 | `#6FA3C4` | `#EBD7AE` | `#E6CF9F` |\n| Noon 12:30 | `#FFF8E8`, 3.6 | `#7FB2CF` | `#F1E3BF` | `#EFE0B5` |\n| Golden 17:30 | `#FFB068`, 2.8 | `#5E7FA0` | `#F0A860` | `#DB9552` |\n| Dusk 19:00 | `#FF8A50`, 1.4 | `#3A4F6E` | `#E07B39` | `#C9733C` |\n| Night 23:30 | `#8FA6D8`, 0.5 | `#0E1726` | `#1E2B45` | `#1A2438` |\n\nNoon: bleached, high contrast, heat shimmer. Night: indigo washes and ink, stars, moon; fire and lamps own the screen.\n\n### 10.4 Fonts\n\nEmbed as base64 woff2 via esbuild's `dataurl` loader, from npm: `@fontsource/bebas-neue` (titles, OFL), `@fontsource/oswald` (UI, OFL), `@fontsource/special-elite` (ledger, tapes, Apache-2.0), `@fontsource/reenie-beanie` (grease pencil on the map, OFL). Latin subset only. `await document.fonts.load()` before drawing any CanvasTexture text.\n\n### 10.5 Asset list (all procedural, in code)\n\n`src/data/prefabs.js` holds data-described prefabs built from a primitive kit (box, cylinder, wedge, corrugated panel, plank stack), with vertex colours, chipped edges, painted decals from CanvasTexture. The engine's own library is fine for generic props: `RoundedBoxGeometry`, `ConvexGeometry` (rocks), `BufferGeometryUtils.mergeGeometries`, `DecalGeometry`, `SkeletonUtils`.\n\n- **Terrain and plants:** terrain chunks; grass tufts (instanced, burnable); acacia (3 shapes), brush, dead tree, rocks, termite mound; reeds.\n- **Town:** shack, tin-roof house, concrete company block, union hall, church and mast, bar with stools and the tab board, market stall, water tower, tank stand, generator, fuel tank, pipe run, conveyor, mine sheds, terraces, strongroom, rail track, signal box, steel bridge (destructible cluster), jetty.\n- **Military:** sandbags, MG nest, watchtower, barrier, shipping container, tent, refugee tents, crates, oil drum (explosive), tire stack.\n- **Signs:** road signs, slogans and posters.\n- **Vehicles:** pickup, technical, cargo truck, skiff, barge, locomotive, wagons, payroll car, bus-sized wreck, patrol boat wreck.\n- **Weapons:** all 9, machete, Molotov, syringe, pliers, wrench, bottle, map, GPS, monocular, recorder, passports, ledger.\n- **Creatures:** zebra, antelope (shared quadruped rig), vulture.\n- **People:** one rig, faction outfits, civilians, the cast (distinct silhouettes: the Widow's headwrap and folding chair, the Comptroller's tie, the Foreman's hard hat, the Doctor's apron, the Notary's cardigan and lamp, four mercs).\n\n**Grass and water notes.** Grass is instanced tufts in 32 m chunks around the camera, dense to about 160 m. Beyond that, a terrain stipple. All blade normals point up for soft cel bands and no noisy edges (✔ that removed the speckle). Water: flat toon bands, a foam line where depth is shallow, and slow noise-driven ripples.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Animation\n\nThree's own `AnimationMixer` is the base; all clips are authored in code as `AnimationClip`s from compact pose tables in `src/anim/`.\n\n- **Humanoid rig** (`rig.js`): about 19 bones (hips, spine, chest, neck, head, shoulders, upper/lower arms, hands, upper/lower legs, feet) with sockets on the hands. Geometry: tapered segments merged into one `SkinnedMesh`, skin weights blended at joints, outfits as extra meshes and vertex colours, faces as bold ink marks.\n- **Clip DSL:** `{ name, dur, loop, keys:[{ t, pose:{ bone:[x,y,z] } }] }`, built into `QuaternionKeyframeTrack`s. Crossfade with `AnimationAction`. Additive layers (`AnimationUtils.makeClipAdditive`, `AdditiveAnimationBlendMode`) for aim, recoil, flinch. Aim pitch is a bone offset after `mixer.update`.\n- **NPC clips (about 30):** idle, smoke, talk, walk, run, crouch walk, aim, fire, reload, clear jam, throw, flinch, fall wounded, crawl, drag comrade, being dragged, panic burn, die front, die back, sit at bar, sleep, wave truck down, inspect papers, kneel, inject, carry case, wave arms at fire.\n- **First-person clips (about 40):**\n  - Idle, fire and reload per weapon class; clear jam per class; gun breaks; machete swings.\n  - Molotov light-and-throw; syringe; the four surgeries; dose from bottle.\n  - Raise and lower map, GPS check; raise and lower monocular; wrench at hood.\n  - Papers held out the window; door and lever; steering left and right, gear; crawl and being dragged; lie down and get up; press Record.\n- **Procedural on top:** weapon sway, breathing, fever tremor, recoil spring, limp.\n- **Hands:** a separate arm rig parented to the camera, scaled small and close so it never clips walls. Camera near 0.05. Per-merc skin and tattoo via vertex colour.\n- **Ragdoll:** at death, swap to about 10 capsules joined with `JointData.spherical` (✔), copy poses back to bones, and freeze after it settles. Keep the newest 60 bodies. Burned corpses turn charred by uniform.\n- **Machines:** wheels (spin, steer, Rapier suspension length), hood and door clips, conveyor, train wheels, mast fall.\n- **Animals:** quadruped rig: graze, walk, gallop, fall. Vultures: glide and flap via vertex shader.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Audio (all code, no files, no samples)\n\n`src/audio/` on Web Audio. Every recipe takes a `BaseAudioContext`, so the same code runs in `OfflineAudioContext` for tests. ✔ Offline rendering works here.\n\n**Buses:** Master → Music, SFX, Voice, UI, with a duck for voices and a low-pass for \"hearing damage\" after near explosions. **Music bus gain = 0.10 by default**, set by the Settings slider. A test reads it on a fresh profile.\n\n**SFX recipes (parameterised, per weapon and surface):**\n- **Guns:** noise transient through band-pass plus a sub thump (sine 120→40 Hz) plus a mechanical click, then a tail through a convolver with a generated IR (plains: long slapback; town: short; pit: boomy).\n- **Distance:** low-pass, and delay by speed of sound for far shots and explosions (`delay = d / 340`).\n- **Jam:** dry click and a hiss of breath. **Explosions:** layered, with debris patter.\n- **Fire:** crackle (filtered noise, random pops), a roar whose level is the count of nearby burning cells, panned to the front's centroid.\n- **Wind and grass:** filtered noise following wind speed. **Birds** by day, **insects** at dusk, frogs by the river, distant artillery.\n- **Vehicles:** detuned saws following RPM, tyre noise by surface, gear clunk, cough when damaged. Skiff outboard. Barge horn. Locomotive.\n- **Body:** footsteps by surface, heartbeat, fever whine with tremolo, syringe, pliers, finger snap.\n- **Locator** beep, map rustle, UI ticks.\n\n**Voices: Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish.** A generator turns each word of the English line into syllables, seeded by the word's hash (so words repeat and it sounds like a language), and speaks them with one voice per speaker: sawtooth through two band-pass formants moving per vowel, noise bursts for consonants, 11–16 syllables/s, pitch contour falling at full stops and rising at question marks. Each cast member has fixed pitch, formant shift and tempo: the Notary low and slow with a dry cough, the Ferryman unhurried, the Comptroller clipped, the Foreman loud, Dao fast, Tomasz too loud and rough. Guards: a family of voices per faction. The Notary's tapes go through a band-limited \"recorder\" filter with hiss.\n\n**Music, composed in code, original.** Pre-render plucks with Karplus-Strong into `AudioBuffer`s (Web Audio's own delay loop can't go short enough for high notes). Palette: thumb-piano ostinato, nylon-string pluck, slide lead by FM glide, bowed drone, frame drum from sine and noise. Don't echo the source's soundtrack.\n\n| Cue | When | Feel |\n|---|---|---|\n| Title | Front end | Slow two-bar ostinato, drone, a slide phrase every 8 bars |\n| Road | Driving | Plucks and hand drum, with long silences |\n| Danger | Alert and combat, by intensity | Drum pulse enters, drone rises |\n| Fever | Fever | Same ostinato detuned and slowing |\n| Fire | Near a big front | Drone and low drum only |\n| Dawn | Waking | Single phrase |\n| Mercy | Mercy scene | Nothing, then one plucked phrase |\n| Ending | Epilogue | Solo pluck, one per ending |\n\nMostly the field is **quiet** and music comes and goes: wind and grass are the score. One vehicle radio cycles music and the Notary's readings.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Technical plan\n\n### 13.1 Stack\n\n`three@0.180.0` with `WebGPURenderer` and TSL ✔; `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3` ✔ (WASM embedded in the JS, so one file works); plain JS modules, DOM UI, Web Audio, Gamepad API; esbuild ✔; Playwright 1.63.0 for tests. `npm install` in the dev folder, and commit `package-lock.json`.\n\n### 13.2 Layout\n\n```\nsrc/\n  main.js         boot, mode routing (standalone, session, test)\n  core/           loop, rng streams, event queue, tunables, save, small fsm helpers\n  data/           meta, world, prefabs, weapons, npcs, contracts, lines, dictations, tunables, shop, attract, features, about.json\n  sim/            index (createSim), terrain, physics, player, weapons, wounds, fever, fire, wind, time, ai/*, posts, vehicles, economy, buddies, factions, contracts, director, job, bot\n  view/           renderer, post, sky, terrain, grass, vegetation, props, water, fire, characters, fpv, vehicles, map, gps, effects\n  anim/           rig, clips (npc, first-person, animal), dsl\n  audio/          engine, sfx, ambience, voice, music\n  ui/             stack and focus, prompts, glyphs, hud, screens/*\n  input/          actions, pad, keyboard, mouse\n  net/            transport, hub, link, protocol, job netcode\n  dev/            devmode, overlays, placement\n  attract/        director\nvendor/session.js  the hub client, unmodified\ntools/            build.mjs, test.mjs, tests/*, hub/ (harness.html, bridge.js, round.js), balance.mjs, validate-data.mjs, shots.mjs\ndocs/             DESIGNER.md, issues-log.md\ntest-output/      what tests leave behind\n```\n\n### 13.3 The one architecture rule: sim and view are separate\n\n- `src/sim` holds all game state and rules. It never touches DOM, canvas or the renderer. It imports only pure code, Rapier and three's math. ✔ `three/webgpu` imports fine in plain Node with no DOM, so `Vector3` and friends can be shared.\n- `const sim = createSim({ scenario, seed, tunables, players })`, `sim.step(inputFrames)` advances 1/60 s, `sim.state` is readable, `sim.drainEvents()` hands view, audio and net their events.\n- View, audio and UI read state and events. They never change state except by input frames and dev commands.\n- This gives you, for free: fast playthrough tests in Node; `AIHub.server` (a sim with nothing else); attract mode (a sim plus a bot); and networked clients. All randomness in the sim goes through named seeded streams.\n- Fixed step with an accumulator, up to 5 catch-up steps per frame. Render interpolates. Under `?test=1` the loop stops and tests call `__game.step(n)`.\n- **Data-driven for designers (TASK.md's \"normal designer tools\").** Everything content-like (places, posts, prefabs, weapons, NPC archetypes, contracts, lines, tunables, attract reel) is plain data in `src/data/`, validated by `tools/validate-data.mjs` and documented in `docs/DESIGNER.md` with short how-tos: add a weapon, a post, a contract, a line, a prefab, a tunable. New content needs no system code.\n\n### 13.4 Rendering recipes (✔ all verified, copy them)\n\n```js\nimport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { pass, mrt, output, normalView, uniform, vec2, vec3, vec4, float, Fn, screenUV, screenSize, time, sin, cos,\n  step, smoothstep, length, dot, max, luminance, hash, floor, fract, perspectiveDepthToViewZ, cameraNear, cameraFar,\n  attribute, texture, positionLocal, positionWorld, positionWorldDirection, mix, color, uv, abs, normalize, cross,\n  cameraPosition, mx_noise_float } from 'three/tsl';\n\nconst renderer = new THREE.WebGPURenderer({ antialias: false });   // falls back to WebGL2 by itself\nrenderer.setPixelRatio(1); renderer.setSize(innerWidth, innerHeight);\nrenderer.shadowMap.enabled = true; renderer.shadowMap.type = THREE.PCFSoftShadowMap;\ndocument.body.appendChild(renderer.domElement);\nawait renderer.init();   // catch: no graphics at all → DOM message, AIHub.fail()\nrenderer.onDeviceLost = showGraphicsLostBanner;                    // sim keeps running; reload restores from the save\n\n// Toon ramp: 3 hard bands. Use everywhere.\nconst ramp = new THREE.DataTexture(new Uint8Array([70,70,70,255, 150,150,150,255, 255,255,255,255]), 3, 1, THREE.RGBAFormat);\nramp.minFilter = ramp.magFilter = THREE.NearestFilter; ramp.needsUpdate = true;\nconst mat = new THREE.MeshToonNodeMaterial({ color: 0x8a3e22, gradientMap: ramp });\n\nscene.backgroundNode = mix(color(0xe07b39), color(0x3a4f6e), smoothstep(0.0, 0.45, positionWorldDirection.y.max(0))); // drive with uniforms\nscene.fog = new THREE.Fog(0xc9733c, 70, 460);\n```\n\n**Ink post chain.** `fever`, `inkAmt`, `hatchAmt`, `grainAmt`, `inkWidth` are uniforms. Extend `warp` and the grade with desaturation, vignette, damage ink and the screen-wipe mask.\n\n```js\nconst scenePass = pass(scene, camera);\nscenePass.setMRT(mrt({ output, nrm: normalView }));\nconst colTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('output'), nrmTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('nrm'), depthTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('depth');\nconst warp = Fn(() => {\n  const d = screenUV.sub(0.5);\n  const wob = vec2(sin(screenUV.y.mul(23).add(time.mul(2.3))), cos(screenUV.x.mul(19).add(time.mul(1.7)))).mul(0.006).mul(fever);\n  return screenUV.add(wob).add(d.mul(length(d)).mul(0.05).mul(fever));\n})();\nconst viewDist = (u) => perspectiveDepthToViewZ(depthTex.sample(u).r, cameraNear, cameraFar).negate();\nconst ink = Fn(() => {\n  const px = vec2(inkWidth).div(screenSize);\n  const zC = viewDist(warp), nC = nrmTex.sample(warp).xyz;\n  const isGeo = step(0.5, length(nC));                     // the sky writes no normal\n  let dz = float(0), dn = float(0);\n  for (const [ox, oy] of [[1,0],[-1,0],[0,1],[0,-1]]) {\n    const u = warp.add(vec2(ox, oy).mul(px));\n    dz = dz.add(viewDist(u).sub(zC).abs().div(zC));\n    dn = dn.add(float(1).sub(dot(nrmTex.sample(u).xyz, nC).clamp(0, 1)));\n  }\n  const near = float(1).sub(smoothstep(60, 220, zC));      // normal edges fade with distance\n  return max(smoothstep(0.05, 0.14, dz), smoothstep(0.5, 1.1, dn).mul(isGeo).mul(near));\n})();\nconst graded = Fn(() => {\n  const c = colTex.sample(warp).rgb, lum = luminance(c), p = screenUV.mul(screenSize);\n  const h1 = step(fract(p.x.add(p.y).mul(0.125)), 0.25).mul(float(1).sub(smoothstep(0.16, 0.30, lum)));\n  const h2 = step(fract(p.x.sub(p.y).mul(0.125)), 0.25).mul(float(1).sub(smoothstep(0.05, 0.16, lum)));\n  const hatch = max(h1, h2).mul(hatchAmt).mul(0.45).mul(step(0.5, length(nrmTex.sample(warp).xyz)));\n  const g = floor(p.mul(0.5));\n  const grain = hash(g.x.add(g.y.mul(1971)).add(floor(time.mul(8)).mul(313))).sub(0.5).mul(grainAmt);\n  return vec4(c.mul(float(1).sub(hatch)).mul(float(1).sub(ink.mul(inkAmt).mul(0.94))).mul(vec3(1.0, 0.97, 0.9)).add(grain), 1);\n})();\nconst post = new THREE.PostProcessing(renderer);\npost.outputNode = graded;\n// each frame: post.render();\n```\n\n**Burnable, swaying grass.** A DataTexture `burnTex` (512², RGBA8: R burnt, G burning, B regrow) mirrors the fire grid. Terrain, grass and trees all sample it.\n\n```js\n// Geometry: a thin ConeGeometry tuft (translate up by half its height, set every normal to 0,1,0), wrapped in an\n// InstancedBufferGeometry: copy index, position, normal and uv; setAttribute('instOffset', new\n// THREE.InstancedBufferAttribute(offsets, 3)); set instanceCount; mesh.frustumCulled = false.\n// windDir = uniform(new THREE.Vector2(1, 0.3)), windStr = uniform(0.5). burnTex rows run with z, as the fire grid does.\nconst toBurnUV = (xz) => xz.div(2048).add(0.5);\nconst off = attribute('instOffset', 'vec3');\nconst h = positionLocal.y.div(1.3);\nconst b = texture(burnTex, toBurnUV(off.xz));\nconst sway = sin(time.mul(2.1).add(off.x.mul(0.31)).add(off.z.mul(0.27))).mul(windStr).mul(h).mul(h).mul(0.5);\ngrassMat.positionNode = positionLocal.mul(vec3(1, float(1).sub(b.r.mul(0.85)), 1))\n  .add(vec3(windDir.x.mul(sway), 0, windDir.y.mul(sway))).add(off);\ngrassMat.colorNode = mix(mix(color(0xa07f3a), color(0xe2c777), h), color(0x14100c), b.r);\nterrainMat.colorNode = mix(color(0xc48d4e), color(0x1b1512), texture(burnTex, toBurnUV(positionWorld.xz)).r);\n```\n\n**Flames and smoke:** instanced, y-locked billboard cards, alpha-cut, stepped tones. Because they write depth, the ink pass outlines them.\n\n```js\n// Geometry: PlaneGeometry(1, 1) translated up by 0.5, wrapped in an InstancedBufferGeometry as above.\nconst inst = attribute('fireInst', 'vec4');            // xyz position, w size\nfireMat = new THREE.MeshBasicNodeMaterial({ side: THREE.DoubleSide });\nfireMat.positionNode = Fn(() => {\n  const toCam = normalize(cameraPosition.sub(inst.xyz).mul(vec3(1, 0, 1)));\n  const right = cross(vec3(0, 1, 0), toCam);\n  return inst.xyz.add(right.mul(positionLocal.x.mul(inst.w))).add(vec3(0, positionLocal.y.mul(inst.w), 0));\n})();\nconst vuv = uv();\nconst flame = Fn(() => {\n  const seed = inst.x.mul(3.1).add(inst.z.mul(1.7));\n  const n = mx_noise_float(vec3(vuv.x.mul(3).add(seed), vuv.y.mul(2.2).sub(time.mul(1.8)), seed)).mul(0.5).add(0.5);\n  return float(1).sub(abs(vuv.x.sub(0.5)).mul(2)).mul(float(1).sub(vuv.y)).mul(1.6).mul(n.add(0.35));\n})();\nfireMat.opacityNode = flame; fireMat.alphaTestNode = float(0.18);\nfireMat.colorNode = mix(color(0xb5260f), mix(color(0xf26a12), color(0xffe08a), step(0.55, flame)), step(0.32, flame)).mul(1.5);\nfireMat.fog = false;\n// Smoke: the same card with the same billboard (w grows with height, stacked in rising columns):\nconst puff = Fn(() => {\n  const seed = inst.x.mul(0.7).add(inst.y.mul(0.4));\n  const n = mx_noise_float(vec3(vuv.mul(2.6).add(seed), time.mul(0.15))).mul(0.5).add(0.5);\n  return float(1).sub(length(vuv.sub(vec2(0.5, 0.5))).mul(2)).add(n.mul(0.45).sub(0.12));\n})();\nsmokeMat.opacityNode = puff; smokeMat.alphaTestNode = float(0.12);\nsmokeMat.colorNode = mix(color(0x2a2522), color(0x5b4d43), step(0.42, puff));\n```\n\nPool about 3000 flame cards on the nearest burning cells, about 600 smoke cards in rising columns (rounded, two-tone, ink-outlined), and embers.\n\n**Pitfalls found the hard way (✔ each one bit the planner):**\n\n- `transformedNormalView` is deprecated in r180. Use `normalView`.\n- TSL `hash()` takes **one float**. Give it a vec2 and you get vertical stripes.\n- `smoothstep(a, b, x)` needs `a < b`. For a falling edge, use `1 − smoothstep`.\n- A loose `.discard()` that isn't part of the material's graph does nothing. Use `opacityNode` + `alphaTestNode`.\n- The sky writes a zero normal: guard normal-edge and hatch tests with `step(0.5, length(n))`, or the sky turns to ink.\n- Drive everything from `uniform()`s. Changing a node graph recompiles.\n- **Keep the light count constant** (pool of point lights at intensity 0 when unused; changing the count recompiles materials).\n- Shadows: one directional light with a 4096 map following the camera, snapped to texels. `three/addons/csm/CSMShadowNode.js` exists if you want more.\n- Toon materials and `InstancedMesh`, skinning, node `positionNode` all work together ✔.\n- Don't use compute nodes for anything that matters: three's WebGL2 fallback must keep working. ✔ With no WebGPU (the hub's default test browser) it draws the whole ink chain with no code change.\n\nPerformance is out of scope, as TASK.md says: add all the detail, lights and effects the scene wants. The only rule here is §6.2 (don't starve the protocol) and don't exhaust memory (20 GB shared, §14.1).\n\n### 13.5 Rapier recipes (✔ all verified)\n\n```js\nimport RAPIER from '@dimforge/rapier3d-compat';\nawait RAPIER.init();               // console.warn \"using deprecated parameters\": harmless, it's Rapier's own\nconst world = new RAPIER.World({ x: 0, y: -9.81, z: 0 }); world.timestep = 1 / 60;\n\n// Heightfield: column-major, x along cols, z along rows, centred on the origin. nrows, ncols are cell counts.\nconst n = 1024, size = 2048, heights = new Float32Array((n + 1) * (n + 1));\nfor (let col = 0; col <= n; col++) for (let row = 0; row <= n; row++)\n  heights[col * (n + 1) + row] = heightAt((col / n - 0.5) * size, (row / n - 0.5) * size);\nworld.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.heightfield(n, n, heights, { x: size, y: 1, z: size }),\n  world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.fixed()));\n\n// Rays: hit.timeOfImpact. They hit the caster's own capsule unless excluded.\nworld.castRay(new RAPIER.Ray(origin, dir), maxToi, true, undefined, undefined, myCollider);\n\n// Character controller\nconst cc = world.createCharacterController(0.02);\ncc.setUp({ x: 0, y: 1, z: 0 }); cc.enableAutostep(0.4, 0.2, true); cc.enableSnapToGround(0.5);\ncc.setMaxSlopeClimbAngle(50 * Math.PI / 180); cc.setMinSlopeSlideAngle(40 * Math.PI / 180);\ncc.setApplyImpulsesToDynamicBodies(true);\nconst body = world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.kinematicPositionBased().setTranslation(x, y, z));\nconst col = world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.capsule(0.6, 0.35), body);\n// each step\ncc.computeColliderMovement(col, desired); const m = cc.computedMovement(), grounded = cc.computedGrounded();\nconst p = body.translation(); body.setNextKinematicTranslation({ x: p.x + m.x, y: p.y + m.y, z: p.z + m.z });\n\n// Raycast vehicle: set forward to +z as below. ✔ 2500 N on each rear wheel reaches about 16 m/s in 5 s\nconst chassis = world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic().setTranslation(x, y, z).setCanSleep(false));\nconst chassisCol = world.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.cuboid(1.0, 0.45, 2.2).setMass(1400), chassis);\nconst veh = world.createVehicleController(chassis);\nveh.indexUpAxis = 1; veh.setIndexForwardAxis = 2;      // yes, that one is a setter named setIndexForwardAxis\nfor (const w of [[-0.9,-0.3,1.5],[0.9,-0.3,1.5],[-0.9,-0.3,-1.5],[0.9,-0.3,-1.5]])\n  veh.addWheel({ x:w[0], y:w[1], z:w[2] }, { x:0, y:-1, z:0 }, { x:-1, y:0, z:0 }, 0.45, 0.42);\nfor (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) { veh.setWheelSuspensionStiffness(i, 28); veh.setWheelMaxSuspensionTravel(i, 0.4);\n  veh.setWheelFrictionSlip(i, 2.2); veh.setWheelSuspensionCompression(i, 3); veh.setWheelSuspensionRelaxation(i, 4); }\n// each step, before world.step(): setWheelEngineForce(rear), setWheelSteering(front), setWheelBrake, then\nveh.updateVehicle(1 / 60, RAPIER.QueryFilterFlags.EXCLUDE_DYNAMIC, undefined, (c) => c.handle !== chassisCol.handle);\n// for wheels in view: wheelRotation(i), wheelSteering(i), wheelSuspensionLength(i), wheelIsInContact(i)\n\n// Ragdoll joint\nworld.createImpulseJoint(RAPIER.JointData.spherical({ x:0, y:-0.3, z:0 }, { x:0, y:0.3, z:0 }), bodyA, bodyB, true);\n\n// Physics overlay: skip the terrain, or you get 3 million vertices\nworld.debugRender(undefined, (c) => c.handle !== terrainHandle);\n```\n\nSnapshots replay exactly (✔ `takeSnapshot` / `World.restoreSnapshot`), but game saves are your own JSON; rebuild physics on load.\n\n### 13.6 Single-file build (✔)\n\n`tools/build.mjs`:\n\n1. Run `esbuild src/main.js --bundle --format=esm --target=es2022 --minify --loader:.woff2=dataurl --outfile=build/bundle.js`.\n2. Fail if the bundle contains `</script`.\n3. Assemble with **string concatenation, not `.replace()`** (bundles contain `$&`):\n\n```html\n<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>…vendor/session.js exactly…</script>\n<title>…</title> …metas… <style>…</style>\n</head><body><script type=\"module\">…bundle…</script></body></html>\n```\n\n4. Write it to the deliverable path TASK.md names (root, beside the dev folder). TASK.md's folder and file names match, so derive it from the dev folder's own name.\n\n✔ With three and Rapier inlined, this came to 3.1 MB, opens on `file://`, makes no network requests, and passes the harness when served. Inline everything; no CDN.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Testing\n\nTASK.md's rule: one command, printing only failures and a one-line count. Rerun only after changing what it covers. Use scripts first, screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. That command is **`node tools/test.mjs`**, with `--sim`, `--ui`, `--hub` to run one tier.\n\n### 14.1 This computer: corrections and facts (✔)\n\n- **TASK.md's three WebGPU flags aren't enough here.** They give an adapter, but the device is destroyed on the first canvas frame, and everything renders blank with no JS error. **Use the hub's flags. These give real pixels, and a plain Playwright screenshot of the WebGPU canvas works:**\n  ```\n  --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader\n  ```\n- Use `chromium.launch({ headless: true, args })` (the headless shell). The full browser (`channel: 'chromium'`, or headed) aborts here with \"Socket path too long\" because of the long `TMPDIR`. Headed under xvfb got no WebGPU adapter anyway.\n- With **no flags** Chromium has no adapter, and three falls back to WebGL2 and still draws (✔). That's how the hub's own check opens games by default, so test that path too: `backend: webgl2`, no errors, screen drawn. WebKit with the iPhone profile also falls back and runs (✔ `backend: webgl2`, `coarse` pointer). Chromium with a Pixel profile has WebGPU and a coarse pointer (✔). Firefox here reported no WebGL2 in its headless run; a graceful message is the only requirement there.\n- Software WebGPU runs at about 0.3–1.5 fps at 1280 × 720 with 60–150k instances. So **never** assert on real-time behaviour through the renderer. Use `__game.step()`, and small viewports unless checking 10-foot layout at 1920 × 1080.\n- Pointer lock is granted after a click in headless, but `movementX/Y` then stay 0 (✔). Test look through the virtual input or synthetic events.\n- `OfflineAudioContext` renders correctly (✔ gain 0.1 peaks at 0.0999). `AudioContext` starts `running` here, but real browsers need a gesture.\n- Everything you make stays in your dev folder, and you read nothing outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside yours.\n- Run one browser at a time. A 4-copy harness run is one browser but heavy. \"Target closed\" means out of memory; lower the load, don't retry blindly.\n- Serve with Node's `http.createServer().listen(0, '127.0.0.1')` inside `tools/test.mjs` (no extra process, no port clash) or `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, and check the page's title before trusting a result. Stop only what you started.\n\n### 14.2 Test hooks\n\n`window.__game` (always present, read-only except under `?test=1`):\n\n- `state()`: compact JSON: screen, focused id, prompts' glyph set, player, weapons with condition, jam state, health, doses, contract step, time, wind, fire counts, attract status, tunables, bus gains, backend, net stats, feature coverage.\n- `step(n)`, `input(frame)`, `dev(cmd, ...args)` (the same commands as the dev menu), and `renderRecipe(name)`, which returns peak, RMS and duration through `OfflineAudioContext`.\n\nPad stub via `addInitScript`, replacing `navigator.getGamepads` with a standard-mapped pad whose buttons and axes tests set, and counting `playEffect` calls.\n\n### 14.3 Tiers\n\n**`--sim` (Node, no browser, fast). Most of the suite lives here.**\n\n- Fire: front speeds at calm, downwind and upwind (§4.5); roads and water stop it unless embers jump; burnt cells persist through save and load; regrowth timing.\n- Guns: jam rate curve; wear; break at 0; looted range; every weapon fires, reloads and clears.\n- Fever: schedule, warning, pill, collapse and wake; no first attack near alerted enemies; doses always obtainable.\n- Heal: bars, regen ceiling, surgery by damage type. Rescue chain and per-day limit; buddy death is permanent; mercy.\n- AI: perception factors (day, night, grass, smoke), fire avoidance, wounded and drag; posts stay clear until dawn and refill by a real truck only with supply.\n- Vehicles: drives 100 m; damage stages; stall; repair. Skiff floats and moves. Papers pass and fail.\n- Economy: `tools/validate-data.mjs` passes; no unreachable contract, line id or prefab.\n- Determinism: two runs, same seed and inputs, same state hash after 5 sim-minutes. No NaN positions, no NPC stuck over 30 s.\n- **Scripted whole-game playthrough (`sim/bot.js`):** a bot playing through **input frames only** from the barge through every contract, twists on, to each of the three endings (re-loading the save before the choice). God mode and the dev Trigger commands are allowed, to force rare events on schedule. No other state edits. Assert world-state consequences at each step. Also a shorter run of The Job with bots in four seats. **Together these must reach F-list coverage of 100%.**\n- `tools/balance.mjs` (not gated): mortal-bot survival, TTKs, jam frequency, money curve. Read it when tuning.\n\n**`--ui` (Playwright, Chromium with the flags above, `file://`).**\n\n- Boots with no page errors or console errors (the Rapier `console.warn` is fine). `backend: webgpu`, then again with no flags: `backend: webgl2`.\n- Every screen reachable with the pad stub, then with keyboard, then with mouse. Focus visible and never lost. Back always works. Mouse hover moves focus. Click activates.\n- Prompt glyphs follow last input. Xbox option swaps to A B X Y.\n- 10-foot audit at 1920 × 1080 on every screen: font sizes, safe area, ≤ 7 focusables.\n- Touch-only (Chromium Pixel profile, tap with `page.touchscreen`): message, Settings button works, no other menu, attract runs. Also WebKit iPhone profile.\n- Attract: starts between 10 and 11 s idle on title; any of key, mouse, button or stick exits with focus intact; loops past a full cycle; coverage ≥ 80%.\n- Dev mode: reachable from Pause; every page opens; a slider changes behaviour live (for example, fire spread); reset restores; hidden in sessions.\n- Audio: fresh profile music bus = 0.10. Every SFX, voice and music recipe renders non-silent, finite, in range. Voice duration tracks text length. The same line gives the same audio. No `<audio>`, no audio data URIs, no media fetches in the bundle.\n- No network requests from the page at all. No `TODO|FIXME|placeholder|stub` in `src/`.\n- Pixels (`page.screenshot` at low res, compare numbers, don't eyeball): ink present (dark-pixel ratio in a range), sky not black, the fire scene has flame colours, fever changes the picture.\n- About & Credits shows every Source Information field and all feedback answers. The exit-interview JSON parses and has every required key, and no value is empty.\n\n**`--hub` (served harness; run exactly what the hub runs, from its `scripts/hub-check.mjs`).** One harness URL per case, against the built HTML copied next to `tools/hub/*` in `test-output/site/`. A case passes when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true.\n\n```\nharness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&play=8000\nharness.html?game=GAME.html&players=3&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&play=8000\nharness.html?game=GAME.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&play=8000\nharness.html?game=GAME.html&players=3&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave&play=8000\nharness.html?game=GAME.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave&play=8000\nharness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=server&play=8000\n```\n\nRun these with the flags from §14.1, and the 4-player case once more with no flags: that is the hub's default browser, where every copy is on WebGL2 in software. Give each page up to 300 s, as the hub does. HARNESS_CLAIM\n\nThen:\n\n- **The game on its own**, as the hub checks it: open it with no flags, press Enter, Space, Enter, Space, and assert no page errors, `AIHub.active === false`, `AIHub.seeded` not true, and a screen that draws (DOM title text alone is enough to pass while the scene loads).\n- **Co-op for real:** two pages in a harness round, scripted, where p1 ignites, p2 sees it; p2 goes down, p1 revives; case reaches the barge; `finish` has `won` and correct scores.\n- **Net budget:** `send()` never returns `false`. Copies stay under 20 messages/s. `s` stays ≤ 1200 bytes.\n- **Link play:** two pages, host and join, 10 s of play, traffic both ways, results shown.\n\nScreenshots for eyes only: a short `node tools/shots.mjs` set at 1920 × 1080 (each time of day, fire, night, menus, map focus, surgery) into `test-output/`. Use it for art passes, not as a test.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Build order and scale\n\nEach milestone ends with its tests passing. Fire and the look come early, because they are the game. Do the title first.\n\n| # | Milestone | Done when |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | Title, scaffold, recipes §13 proven in your folder, `tools/build.mjs`, `tools/test.mjs` (real from the start) | Bundle opens on `file://`, WebGPU backend, ink chain draws, one sim test and one harness run pass |\n| 1 | Sim core, terrain, physics, player, world data, chunks, sky and time, grass, trees | Walk the whole map in ink-shaded savanna, dawn to night |\n| 2 | **Fire and wind**, burn texture, flames, smoke, cloth strip | Speed tests pass; a lit field looks like the target image |\n| 3 | Weapons, condition, jams, first-person hands and clips, machete, Molotov; NPC rig and clips; AI; posts; ragdolls | Clear a post, get jammed, see a man crawl |\n| 4 | Health, surgery, fever, buddies, rescue, mercy | All trigger from dev commands |\n| 5 | Pickup, technical, truck, skiff; damage and repair; map, GPS, locator, monocular | Drive with the map up; find a case |\n| 6 | Economy, gun table, safehouses, bed, save and load, papers, ceasefire | Buy, sleep, reload a save |\n| 7 | UI system, glyphs, title, pause, settings, HUD, notebook, dev mode, tunables | All UI tests pass on pad, keyboard, mouse |\n| 8 | Contract runner and **all content**, Director, cast, the writing, the world's people and animals | Whole-game bot playthrough passes, all 3 endings |\n| 9 | Audio: buses, SFX, voices, music | Audio tests pass, music at 10% |\n| 10 | Attract reel; touch-only flow | Attract tests pass |\n| 11 | The Job, netcode, card, hub boot, link play | All `--hub` tests pass |\n| 12 | Polish from screenshots at 1920 × 1080; about.json; exit-interview; final build; cleanup | §16 |\n\n**Scale knobs.** If you run short, cut *quantity* from this list, and remove the cut thing entirely:\n\n| Item | Plan | Floor |\n|---|---|---|\n| Contracts | C1–C8, E1, E2 | C1, C2, C3, E1, C4, one of C5/C6, C7, C8 |\n| Weapons | 9 | 6 (`pistol`, `flare`, `carbine`, one of `rifle`/`pump`, one of `belt`/`tube`, `torch`) |\n| Posts | 8 | 4 |\n| Cases / tapes | 12 / 6 | 6 / 4 |\n| Attract vignettes | 14 | 10 (≥ 80% of what remains of the F-list) |\n| Kiln buildings | 16–20 | 8 |\n| Scatter counts, NPC variety, music cues | As listed | Half |\n\n**Never cut:** fire and wind, jams and rust, surgery, fever, rescue and mercy, map, GPS and locator, truck repair, a boat, papers, sleep and day/night, a buddy twist, all three endings, The Job in hub sessions, dev mode with sliders, attract, 10-foot UI with glyph swap, About & Credits.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Done checklist\n\nBefore you finish:\n\n1. `node tools/test.mjs` passes: one line, zero failures. Do a last read of `docs/issues-log.md`.\n2. The deliverable HTML is in the root as TASK.md names it, with the session client first and unmodified, works on `file://` and when served, and makes no network requests.\n3. No TODOs, placeholders or stubs. Everything in the menus works. Every feature reachable.\n4. A fresh profile really has music at 10%.\n5. `src/data/about.json` is final: Source Information (date, model and environment copied from your TASK.md; `Plan` is this file's name; `Usage` reads \"added on import\"), and honest answers to every Testing, Engine and Prompt question. About & Credits shows them. The exit-interview JSON sits beside the HTML and parses. **Also put all of it in your final chat reply, as TASK.md requires.**\n6. `docs/DESIGNER.md` is true.\n7. Delete `node_modules/` and `test-output/` if they aren't needed to review the run (`npm ci` restores them). Stop any process you started. Leave nothing in `/tmp`.\n\n### Where each TASK.md item is covered\n\n| TASK.md | Here |\n|---|---|\n| Source, Goal, Scope (original, feature-complete, vertical slice) | §1, §2, §3, §5, §13.3 |\n| Genre, Perspective | §1 |\n| Game Title | §0 (builder's) |\n| Research | §0 |\n| Package (single HTML), Working Environment (dev folder, no outside reads, ports, PIDs) | §13.6, §14.1 |\n| Tech Stack (r180, WebGPU, ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad) | §13, §7.2, §12 |\n| Art Direction | §10 |\n| Animation | §11 |\n| Audio (code only, gibberish voices, music at 10%) | §12 |\n| Online, Multiplayer (hub and on its own) | §6 |\n| Performance | §13.4 |\n| Testing (one command, whole-game playthrough, no audit agents) | §14 |\n| Dev mode, sliders, reset | §9 |\n| Attract Mode | §8 |\n| Narrative tone | §5.1 |\n| UI & UX, Touch-only | §7 |\n| Done | §15, §16 |\n| Source Information, Testing/Engine/Prompt Feedback, exit interview, `aiHub` block | §7.5, §16 |","numLines":520,"startLine":669,"totalLines":1189}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012HmpyDuqUoHox7eNMqAWxH","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
